# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 531 money in code
T.append(topic(531, 'money-in-code', R, 27, 'Money in Code: Minor Units, Rounding and Currency',
 ['Carry amounts as integer minor units with a currency code, in storage and in JSON',
  'Round once, at a stated step, with a stated rule, and allocate remainders so splits add up',
  'Convert currencies with a recorded rate, and make money endpoints reject a reused key with a changed amount'],
 2, 14, [224],
 [text(531, 1, 'Amounts on the wire and in the database',
   "A binary double cannot hold 0.1 exactly, so 0.1 + 0.2 gives 0.30000000000000004, and many JSON parsers, including JavaScript’s, read every number as a double. Store money as an integer count of the currency’s minor unit (₹499.99 is 49,999 paise) or as a fixed-scale decimal, and send it the same way: {\"amount\": 49999, \"currency\": \"INR\"}, or a decimal string such as \"499.99\". The ISO 4217 code says how many minor digits to use: INR and USD have 2, JPY has 0, and KWD has 3.",
   ['minor-units']),
  text(531, 2, 'Worked example: splitting ₹100 three ways',
   "₹100 is 10,000 paise, and 10,000 ÷ 3 is 3,333 with 1 paisa left over. Rounding each share to ₹33.33 charges ₹99.99 in total, so one paisa vanishes from the books. Instead give everyone the floor (3,333) and hand the leftover units out one at a time by a fixed rule, such as to the payer first: 3,334 + 3,333 + 3,333 = 10,000. The shares differ by at most one paisa and always add up.",
   ['allocation', 'rounding']),
  diagram(531, 3, 'From request to stored amount',
   'The amount is made exact at the edge and rounded only where a rule says so.', ['minor-units', 'rounding'],
   ['The request carries {"amount": "499.99", "currency": "INR"}',
    'Look up INR’s minor digits in ISO 4217: 2',
    'Parse the string exactly into 49,999 paise; reject a third decimal place',
    'Apply tax or discounts in integers and exact decimals, rounding once with the named mode',
    'Store 49,999 with INR; format as ₹499.99 only when displaying']),
  text(531, 4, 'Rounding and conversion: choose the rule and the moment',
   "Two decisions change totals. The mode: half-up turns 2.5 into 3, while half-even (banker’s rounding) turns 2.5 into 2 and 3.5 into 4, which removes the upward bias over millions of roundings. The moment: three items at ₹10.10 with 18% tax give 3 × ₹1.82 = ₹5.46 if tax is rounded per line, but ₹5.45 if it is computed once on ₹30.30. Currency conversion follows the same rule: multiply the source minor units by the rate in exact decimal, round once into the target’s minor units, and store the rate and source amount alongside the result.",
   ['rounding', 'conversion']),
  text(531, 5, 'Trap: a retry that changes the amount',
   "Idempotency keys stop a retried payment from charging twice, but only if the server checks that the retry is the same request. If a client bug reuses the key for a ₹5,000 payout after a ₹500 one, replaying the stored ₹500 result hides the bug, and running the new request breaks the key’s promise. Store a hash of the request body with the key, replay only when it matches, and reject a mismatch with an error. The IETF Idempotency-Key draft suggests 422 Unprocessable Content for this case, and Stripe’s API, for example, also returns an error when a key is reused with different parameters.",
   ['idempotent-money-apis'])],
 [mc('t531-a1', 'minor-units', 'Which JSON body is safest for a payment amount sent to an API?',
   [('a', '{"amount": 499.99}'), ('b', '{"amount": 49999, "currency": "INR"}'),
    ('c', '{"amount": "₹499.99"}'), ('d', '{"amount": 499.99, "currency": "INR"}')], 'b',
   'a: a JSON number like 499.99 is read as a double by many parsers, and the currency is missing. b: correct, an integer count of paise is exact in every parser up to 2^53, and the code says what the unit is. c: a formatted display string depends on locale (“1,234.50” or “1.234,50”) and has to be parsed by guesswork. d: the currency helps, but the amount is still a float on the way in.'),
  mc('t531-a2', 'minor-units', 'An API stores amounts for INR and JPY accounts as integer minor units. What must travel with each amount?',
   [('a', 'The exchange rate on the day it was created'), ('b', 'Its ISO 4217 currency code'),
    ('c', 'A flag saying whether it was rounded'), ('d', 'How many decimals the app displays')], 'b',
   'a: a rate is needed only when converting, not to know what the stored number means. b: correct, the code tells you the unit, and INR has 2 minor digits while JPY has 0, so 500 means ₹5.00 or ¥500. c: a rounding flag does not say what the unit is. d: display settings vary by screen; the currency code is the stable fact.'),
  mc('t531-a3', 'allocation', '₹100 is split between 3 people in paise. Which result keeps the books balanced?',
   [('a', '3,333 paise each, writing off the extra paisa'), ('b', '3,334 + 3,333 + 3,333 paise'),
    ('c', '3,333.33 paise each, kept as decimals'), ('d', '3,334 paise each, the payer covering 2 more')], 'b',
   'a: the books are short by one paisa on every split. b: correct, floors plus one leftover unit handed out by a fixed rule sum to exactly 10,000. c: a paisa cannot be charged in thirds, so this only postpones the rounding. d: charging 10,002 paise collects more than the bill.'),
  mc('t531-a4', 'rounding', 'Half-even (banker’s) rounding is applied to ₹2.5 and ₹3.5, rounding to whole rupees. What are the results?',
   [('a', '3 and 4'), ('b', '2 and 4'), ('c', '2 and 3'), ('d', '3 and 3')], 'b',
   'a: that is half-up, which always rounds .5 upwards. b: correct, half-even rounds .5 to the nearest even digit, so the ups and downs cancel out over many values. c: that is half-down or truncation. d: that is half-odd, a rarely used mode.'),
  mc('t531-a5', 'idempotent-money-apis', 'A client retries POST /payouts with the same Idempotency-Key, but the amount has changed from ₹500 to ₹5,000. What should the server do?',
   [('a', 'Pay ₹5,000, since the latest request wins'), ('b', 'Replay the stored ₹500 result as a success'),
    ('c', 'Reject it: the key belongs to another request'), ('d', 'Pay the ₹4,500 difference as a top-up')], 'c',
   'a: running a new request under an old key breaks the promise that one key means one operation. b: the client believes ₹5,000 went out when only ₹500 did, and the bug stays hidden. c: correct, a key names one request; comparing a stored hash of the body exposes the client bug at once. d: inventing a top-up guesses at intent and moves money nobody asked for.'),
  order('t531-a6', 'conversion', 'Order the steps for charging an INR card for a $12.99 price.',
   ['Parse "12.99" USD into 1,299 cents', 'Fetch the USD-to-INR rate and record it with its time',
    'Multiply exactly: 1,299 × 83.2715 = 108,169.6785 paise', 'Round once, half-even, to 108,170 paise',
    'Store 108,170 INR with the rate and the 1,299 USD source amount'],
   ['s3', 's1', 's5', 's2', 's4'],
   'Parse into exact units first, use one recorded rate, keep full precision through the multiplication and round only at the end, then store enough to repeat and explain the conversion later.'),
  est('t531-a7', 'rounding', 'An app splits ₹100 bills three ways, rounds each share to ₹33.33 and drops the leftover paisa. It does 200,000 such splits a day. How many rupees go unaccounted for each day?', '₹', 2000,
   ['One paisa is lost per split', '100 paise make a rupee'], '200,000 × 1 paisa ÷ 100', '200,000 paise ÷ 100 = ₹2,000 a day',
   'Sanity check: that is about ₹7.3 lakh a year of drift from one rounding rule, which reconciliation will flag even though each single split looks fine.', 0.1, [200000, 20000])],
 flash(531, [('How should money travel and be stored?', 'As integer minor units (or a decimal string or fixed-scale decimal) plus an ISO 4217 currency code.'),
             ('Half-even rounding of 2.5 and 3.5?', '2 and 4: .5 goes to the even neighbour, so bias cancels out.'),
             ('Same idempotency key, different amount?', 'Reject it: a key names one request, so a changed body is a client bug, not a retry.')]),
 src([('PostgreSQL: Numeric types', 'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-numeric.html'),
      ('RFC 8259: JSON, section 6 on numbers and interoperability', 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259'),
      ('IETF draft: The Idempotency-Key HTTP Header Field', 'https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-httpapi-idempotency-key-header/'),
      ('Martin Fowler: Money (Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture)', 'https://martinfowler.com/eaaCatalog/money.html')]),
 deep(['minor-units', 'rounding', 'allocation', 'conversion', 'idempotent-money-apis'], 'How money stays exact from request to storage',
  ["Parse money at the edge into an exact type. A request carrying “499.99” or 49999 with “INR” becomes 49,999 minor units with the code INR, using the number of minor digits that ISO 4217 lists for the currency; a value with more decimals than the currency allows is rejected, not rounded silently. From then on, adding and subtracting are integer operations, which are exact.",
   "Multiplication and division are where rounding enters: tax, discounts, interest, conversion and splits. Each produces a fraction of a minor unit that has to be rounded. Do it once, at a step you name (per line or per invoice), with a mode you name (half-up, half-even), and keep the unrounded rate or price in the record so the calculation can be repeated and explained later.",
   "Splits need allocation, not rounding. Dividing 10,000 paise three ways gives three floors of 3,333 and a remainder of 1. Hand the remainder out one unit at a time by a deterministic rule, such as largest fractional part first or a fixed party order. The same rule, applied on refund, reverses each share exactly, so a partially refunded group bill does not leave a stray paisa behind.",
   "Conversion uses one recorded rate. Fetch the rate once for the operation, keep it as an exact decimal with enough digits (often 6 or more), multiply the source minor units by it, and round once into the target’s minor units. Store the source amount and currency, the rate, its source and time, and the result, so a refund can reverse the same conversion rather than using today’s rate by accident.",
   "Endpoints that move money are idempotent and strict about it. The idempotency key is stored with a hash of the request body and the result, in the same transaction as the money write. A matching retry gets the stored result; a mismatched body under the same key is rejected. The bookkeeping itself, balanced ledger rows and reconciliation, is an architecture topic of its own; this lesson stops at the API and the stored amount."],
  ['Any system that charges, refunds, splits, converts or reports money.', 'Public payment and payout APIs, where clients in many languages parse your amounts.'],
  ['Avoid floats for money anywhere, including JSON payloads and analytics totals that finance will compare.', 'Avoid converting with a fresh rate on refund; reuse the rate stored with the original charge.'],
  ['Representation', 'Exactness', 'Main pitfall'],
  [['Integer minor units', 'Exact for add and subtract', 'Needs the currency to know the unit'],
   ['Decimal string or fixed-scale decimal', 'Exact to its stated scale', 'Scale must cover rates and tax'],
   ['Binary float', 'Approximate', 'Totals drift by fractions of a paisa']],
  'Per-line or per-invoice tax rounding',
  ['3 items at ₹10.10 each', '18% tax', 'Round half-up to the paisa'],
  ['Per line: ₹10.10 × 0.18 = ₹1.818, which rounds to ₹1.82.',
   'Three lines: 3 × ₹1.82 = ₹5.46 of tax.',
   'Per invoice: ₹30.30 × 0.18 = ₹5.454, which rounds to ₹5.45.',
   'The two rules differ by 1 paisa on this invoice; across many invoices the gap adds up and must match what the tax return reports.'],
  'Neither rule is wrong; mixing them is. Pick the one the rules or contract require and use it in invoices, credit notes and reports alike.',
  'Paise, GST lines and a dollar price', 'Illustrative example',
  "Illustrative: an Indian marketplace shows ₹499.99 on screen but its API sends 49999 with INR and stores the same. Tax on each invoice is computed either per line or on the invoice total, and the business picks one rule and applies it to invoices, credit notes and refunds so the totals reconcile with its tax filings. For a seller who prices in dollars, checkout converts once with a stored rate and keeps both amounts, so a refund a month later returns exactly what was charged. Retried payment calls carry an idempotency key, and a key reused with a different amount is rejected and logged as a client bug.",
  [('“A double is precise enough for rupees.”', '“Doubles cannot hold 0.1 exactly; I’d carry paise as integers with the currency code, in JSON too.”'),
   ('“Round each share and the totals will work out.”', '“I’d allocate the remainder by a fixed rule so the shares always sum to the bill.”')],
  ['Carry money as integer minor units with an ISO 4217 code, on the wire and at rest.', 'Round once, at a named step, with a named mode.',
   'Convert with one recorded rate and keep it with the result.', 'Reject an idempotency key reused with a different body.']),
 {"kind": "predict", "title": "Splitting ₹100 three ways", "prompt": "A bill-split feature computes 100 ÷ 3 in floating point, rounds each share to two decimals and charges three cards. What does finance see when it adds up the charges for this bill?",
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "₹99.99 charged against a ₹100 bill", "feedback": "Right: each share rounds down to ₹33.33, and the leftover paisa belongs to nobody."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "Exactly ₹100, since the rounding cancels out", "feedback": "All three shares round the same way, so nothing cancels; the error is one paisa every time."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "₹100.02, since each share rounds up", "feedback": "33.333… is below the halfway point, so half-up rounding takes it down, not up."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "Work in paise and allocate: 10,000 ÷ 3 gives three floors of 3,333 and a remainder of 1, which goes to one person by a fixed rule. The shares become 3,334, 3,333 and 3,333 and always add up to the bill."},
 see([(224, 'Idempotency keys are what stop a retried payment from posting twice'),
      (240, 'How numbers are encoded in JSON and other formats decides whether amounts survive the trip')])))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 711 dates, time zones and daylight saving
T.append(topic(711, 'time-zones-in-business-logic', R, 28, 'Dates, Time Zones and Daylight Saving in Business Logic',
 ['Store past instants in UTC and keep the IANA zone for anything tied to a person’s or a place’s calendar',
  'Schedule future local events from a local time plus zone, not a precomputed UTC instant',
  'Handle daylight-saving gaps and overlaps, and compute “today” in the zone that defines it'],
 2, 13, [355],
 [text(711, 1, 'Instants, local times and zones',
   "An instant is one point on the timeline, such as when a payment was captured; store it in UTC, for example in PostgreSQL’s timestamptz, which normalises to UTC. A local date and time like “09:00 on 3 March” is not an instant until you add a zone. A zone is an IANA identifier such as Asia/Kolkata or America/New_York, not an offset like +05:30: offsets change with daylight saving and with law, while the identifier carries the rules. India uses +05:30 all year; New York switches between −05:00 and −04:00.",
   ['instant-vs-local']),
  text(711, 2, 'Worked example: a 9 a.m. reminder across a clock change',
   "A user in New York sets a daily 09:00 reminder in January, and the service stores it as 14:00 UTC. That works until 8 March 2026, when New York moves to UTC−4: 14:00 UTC is now 10:00 local, an hour late, every day until November. Store the rule instead, 09:00 with zone America/New_York, and compute each run’s UTC instant from it: 14:00 UTC in winter, 13:00 UTC in summer.",
   ['future-local-events']),
  diagram(711, 3, 'Scheduling the next run of a local-time job',
   'The stored rule stays in local time; only the next run becomes a UTC instant.', ['future-local-events', 'dst-gaps'],
   ['Stored: 09:00 every day, zone America/New_York',
    'Take the next local date: 9 March 2026',
    'Ask the time-zone database for that date’s offset: UTC−4 after the 8 March change',
    '09:00 local becomes 13:00 UTC; enqueue the job for that instant',
    'After it runs, repeat from the next local date, never by adding 24 hours']),
  text(711, 4, 'Gaps and overlaps',
   "On a spring-forward day, US clocks jump from 02:00 to 03:00, so 02:30 does not exist. On the autumn day, clocks go back from 02:00 to 01:00, so 01:30 happens twice. Libraries resolve both with a policy: for a gap, usually move forward; for an overlap, pick the earlier or later offset. Choose on purpose: a nightly job at 02:30 should still run once on the gap day, and one at 01:30 should run once, not twice, on the overlap day. Those days are also 23 and 25 hours long, so “add one day” and “add 24 hours” give different answers.",
   ['dst-gaps']),
  text(711, 5, 'Trap: “today” in the server’s zone',
   "A daily spending limit resets at midnight. If the code takes the date from the server clock in UTC, an Indian user’s day starts at 05:30 IST, so a purchase at 03:00 IST on 5 March counts towards 4 March. Compute business dates in the zone that defines them, whether the user’s, the merchant’s or the one a contract names, and store that zone with the record. Run servers in UTC and never let a result depend on the host’s zone setting.",
   ['business-dates'])],
 [mc('t711-a1', 'instant-vs-local', 'A payment was captured. What should the database store for when it happened?',
   [('a', 'The local time in the user’s zone, no zone kept'), ('b', 'The UTC instant, in a zone-aware timestamp type'),
    ('c', 'The server’s local time, without any zone'), ('d', 'Only the calendar date, as YYYY-MM-DD')], 'b',
   'a: without the zone, nobody can tell later which instant it was, or order it against other events. b: correct, a past event is a fixed instant, and UTC in a zone-aware type compares and sorts correctly everywhere. c: the server’s zone can change with a migration, and the value carries no record of it. d: a date alone loses the order of events and the time needed for disputes.'),
  mc('t711-a2', 'instant-vs-local', 'A user in New York picks their time zone in settings. What should the service save?',
   [('a', '−05:00, the offset on the day they signed up'), ('b', 'EST, the abbreviation shown in the app'),
    ('c', 'America/New_York, the IANA zone name'), ('d', 'The hours between their zone and the server’s')], 'c',
   'a: New York is −04:00 for most of the year, so a fixed offset is wrong for months at a time. b: abbreviations are ambiguous (CST is used for US Central, China and Cuba) and carry no daylight-saving rules. c: correct, the identifier carries the rules, past and future, through the time-zone database. d: it ties the user’s data to wherever the server happens to run.'),
  mc('t711-a3', 'future-local-events', 'A 09:00 weekly meeting for a London team was saved in January as 09:00 UTC. What happens after UK clocks go forward on 29 March 2026?',
   [('a', 'It starts at 10:00 local, an hour late'), ('b', 'It starts at 08:00 local, an hour early'),
    ('c', 'It stays at 09:00, as UTC adjusts itself'), ('d', 'It is skipped on the day of the change')], 'a',
   'a: correct, in summer London is UTC+1, so 09:00 UTC is 10:00 local. b: that would need London to move behind UTC, which it never does. c: UTC never changes; that is why a fixed UTC instant drifts against local time. d: an instant set in UTC always exists; only local times can be skipped.'),
  mc('t711-a4', 'dst-gaps', 'A job runs at 02:30 New York time every night. What happens on 8 March 2026, when clocks jump from 02:00 to 03:00?',
   [('a', 'The time does not exist; a policy must move it'), ('b', 'It runs twice, once at each 02:30'),
    ('c', 'It runs at 02:30 UTC that night instead'), ('d', 'Nothing: every local time exists every day')], 'a',
   'a: correct, 02:00 to 02:59 is skipped that night, so the library or your code must choose, usually 03:00. b: repeated times happen on the autumn change, not the spring one. c: nothing converts a local schedule to UTC on its own. d: on spring-forward days an hour of local time is missing.'),
  mc('t711-a5', 'business-dates', 'An app on UTC servers resets each user’s daily limit at server midnight. When does the limit reset for users in India?',
   [('a', 'At 00:00 IST'), ('b', 'At 05:30 IST'), ('c', 'At 18:30 IST'), ('d', 'At 23:30 IST')], 'b',
   'a: that would need the reset computed in Asia/Kolkata, not in UTC. b: correct, India is UTC+05:30, so midnight UTC is 05:30 IST. c: that subtracts the offset instead of adding it; 18:30 IST is 13:00 UTC. d: no offset between UTC and IST gives this.'),
  order('t711-a6', 'future-local-events', 'Order how a service computes the next run of “09:00 daily, America/New_York”.',
   ['Read the stored local time and zone', 'Pick the next local calendar date',
    'Look up that date’s UTC offset in the time-zone database', 'Convert 09:00 on that date to a UTC instant',
    'Enqueue the job at that instant'],
   ['s4', 's2', 's5', 's1', 's3'],
   'The rule stays local; the offset is looked up for the specific date, so the conversion is right on both sides of a clock change.'),
  est('t711-a7', 'dst-gaps', 'New York’s clocks go back from 02:00 to 01:00 on 1 November 2026. How many hours long is that local day, midnight to midnight?', 'hours', 25,
   ['Local midnight to the next local midnight', 'One hour of local time repeats'], '24 + 1 repeated hour', '24 hours of clock time + the repeated hour from 01:00 to 02:00 = 25 hours',
   'Sanity check: the spring-forward day is 23 hours, so a job that adds 24 hours to its last run drifts an hour at each change.', 0.02, [24, 23])],
 flash(711, [('Instant or local time?', 'An instant is one point on the timeline, stored in UTC; a local time needs a zone to become one.'),
             ('Store an offset or a zone?', 'The IANA zone, such as Asia/Kolkata; offsets change with daylight saving and with law.'),
             ('Future 09:00 local event?', 'Store 09:00 plus the zone and compute each UTC instant when it is needed.')]),
 src([('IANA Time Zone Database', 'https://www.iana.org/time-zones'),
      ('PostgreSQL: Date/Time types', 'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-datetime.html'),
      ('RFC 3339: Date and Time on the Internet: Timestamps', 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339')]),
 deep(['instant-vs-local', 'future-local-events', 'dst-gaps', 'business-dates'], 'How a service handles time from request to schedule',
  ["At the API edge, timestamps arrive as RFC 3339 strings with an offset, such as 2026-03-05T09:00:00+05:30, and become instants. A string with no offset is either rejected or read in a zone the API documents, never silently in the server’s. Responses send instants in UTC or with an explicit offset.",
   "Instants are stored in UTC in a zone-aware type, and servers run with UTC as their zone, so nothing depends on where a container happens to start. Durations, such as a token valid for 3,600 seconds or a timeout, are arithmetic on instants and need no zone.",
   "Anything defined by a person’s or a place’s calendar is stored as a local date or time plus an IANA zone: a recurring reminder, a shop’s opening hours, a billing period that ends at local midnight, a flight’s departure. It becomes an instant only when needed, using the time-zone rules current at that moment.",
   "Those rules change several times a year as governments move or abolish daylight saving. Keep the time-zone database in the operating system, runtime and database up to date, and recompute stored future instants after an update; this is the second reason not to precompute UTC instants for local events.",
   "Calendar arithmetic happens in local terms: “tomorrow at 09:00” adds a calendar day and then converts. Gaps and overlaps are resolved by an explicit policy, and tests cover one spring-forward and one fall-back date in a zone that observes daylight saving, not just Asia/Kolkata, which does not."],
  ['Reminders, schedules, opening hours, billing periods and daily limits.', 'Any product with users in more than one zone, or in one that observes daylight saving.'],
  ['Avoid storing future local events as fixed UTC instants.', 'Avoid relying on the server’s zone, or treating offsets such as +05:30 as zones.'],
  ['Value', 'Store as', 'Example'],
  [['A past event', 'UTC instant', 'payment captured_at'],
   ['A future local event', 'Local time + IANA zone', '09:00, America/New_York'],
   ['A business date', 'Date + the zone that defines it', 'statement date in Asia/Kolkata'],
   ['A duration', 'Seconds or an interval', 'token valid for 3,600 s']],
  'How long is a day?',
  ['New York, which observes daylight saving', '8 March 2026: clocks go from 02:00 to 03:00', '1 November 2026: clocks go from 02:00 back to 01:00'],
  ['On an ordinary day, midnight to midnight is 24 hours.',
   'On 8 March, one local hour is skipped, so the day is 23 hours.',
   'On 1 November, one local hour repeats, so the day is 25 hours.',
   'A job that adds 24 hours to its last run moves an hour on the clock at each change; one that adds a calendar day stays at 09:00.'],
  'Calendar days and 24-hour durations are different units. Use calendar arithmetic in the local zone for schedules, and durations for timeouts and expiries.',
  'A daily cashback cap for users in India and the Gulf', 'Illustrative example',
  "Illustrative: a payments app caps cashback at ₹500 per user per day. Its servers run in UTC, and the first version reset the cap at UTC midnight, which is 05:30 in India and 04:00 in Dubai, so late-night shoppers found part of the next day’s cap already used. The fix stored each user’s IANA zone (Asia/Kolkata, Asia/Dubai), computed the business date in that zone for every transaction, and kept the instant in UTC for audit. Neither zone observes daylight saving today, but the team still stored the zone rather than a fixed offset, so a future rule change needs only a time-zone database update.",
  [('“We store everything in UTC, so time zones are solved.”', '“UTC is right for past instants; for future local events I’d store the local time and IANA zone and convert when needed.”'),
   ('“Add 24 hours to get tomorrow’s run.”', '“I’d add a calendar day in the user’s zone, because days around clock changes are 23 or 25 hours.”')],
  ['Store past instants in UTC in a zone-aware type.', 'Store future local events as local time plus IANA zone.',
   'Choose gap and overlap policies and test both dates.', 'Compute business dates in the zone that defines them, never the server’s.']),
 {"kind": "predict", "title": "Scheduling by adding 24 hours", "prompt": "A reminder service schedules each next run by adding 24 hours to the last one. A user in New York set it for 09:00 in February. When does it fire on 9 March 2026, after the clocks went forward on 8 March?",
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "At 10:00 local, an hour late", "feedback": "Right: 8 March had only 23 hours, so 24 hours after 09:00 on 7 March is 10:00 on 8 March, and every run after keeps that hour."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "At 09:00 local, as the user set it", "feedback": "That needs calendar arithmetic in the user’s zone; a fixed 24-hour step ignores the clock change."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "At 08:00 local, an hour early", "feedback": "Spring forward removes an hour, so a fixed 24-hour step lands later on the clock, not earlier."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "Store 09:00 with America/New_York and compute each run from the next calendar date in that zone. Durations suit timeouts and expiries; calendar arithmetic suits schedules."},
 see([(355, 'Wall clocks and monotonic clocks: which one to read for timestamps and which for durations'),
      (227, 'Scheduled jobs that run at a local time are enqueued by a scheduler like this'),
      (240, 'Timestamps in APIs travel as RFC 3339 strings with an explicit offset')])))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 712 text, unicode and encodings
T.append(topic(712, 'text-and-unicode-in-a-backend', R, 29, 'Text in a Backend: Unicode, Normalisation and Length Limits',
 ['Tell bytes, code points and user-perceived characters apart, and name the unit of every limit',
  'Normalise text before comparing it or making it unique, such as usernames and emails',
  'Prevent corrupted text from wrong encodings, cut characters and narrow database character sets'],
 2, 13, [234],
 [text(712, 1, 'Bytes, code points and characters',
   "UTF-8 stores each Unicode code point in 1 to 4 bytes: “a” takes 1, “é” 2, “₹” and Devanagari letters such as “न” 3, and most emoji 4. What a reader sees as one character can be several code points: “नि” is “न” plus the vowel sign “ि”, and a family emoji joins several emoji with invisible joiners. So “length” has at least three answers: bytes for storage and wire limits, code points for many databases’ VARCHAR(n), and grapheme clusters for what the user counts.",
   ['text-units']),
  text(712, 2, 'Worked example: one name, two encodings',
   "“José” can be stored with “é” as one code point (U+00E9, the NFC form) or as “e” followed by a combining acute accent (U+0065 U+0301, the NFD form). They look identical but compare unequal, so a uniqueness check lets “José” register twice and a search for one misses the other. The NFC form is 4 code points and 5 bytes; the NFD form is 5 code points and 6 bytes. Normalise to one form, usually NFC, at the edge, before storing, comparing or hashing.",
   ['normalisation']),
  diagram(712, 3, 'Text from request to storage',
   'Bytes become text once, are normalised once, and every later step works on the same form.', ['normalisation', 'identifiers'],
   ['Decode the request body as UTF-8; reject invalid byte sequences',
    'Normalise the text to NFC',
    'For identifiers, also build a case-folded key and check it against lookalike rules',
    'Check each limit in the unit the rule names, such as 30 visible characters',
    'Store in a UTF-8 column; compare and index the normalised key']),
  text(712, 4, 'Identifiers: usernames and emails',
   "For uniqueness, “Asha”, “asha” and “ASHA” are usually meant to be one account. Keep what the user typed for display, plus a canonical key (normalised, then case-folded) with the unique index on the key. Case-folding is not the same as lowercasing: under full case folding the German “ß” becomes “ss”. Lookalikes such as Latin “a” and Cyrillic “а” let “pаypal” pass a uniqueness check, so restrict usernames to one script or check confusables as Unicode TS #39 describes. For email, the domain is case-insensitive; RFC 5321 lets the local part be case-sensitive, but most providers ignore case, so many services compare a lowercased copy.",
   ['identifiers']),
  text(712, 5, 'Trap: cutting text by bytes',
   "Truncating at a byte count can split a 3-byte character, leaving invalid UTF-8: some databases reject the row, and others store a replacement character “�”. A 100-byte limit on a Hindi name allows only about 33 code points. Truncate on grapheme cluster boundaries and size byte limits for 4-byte characters. On MySQL, the legacy utf8 character set (utf8mb3) holds at most 3 bytes per character, so emoji are rejected or mangled; use utf8mb4.",
   ['text-units', 'encoding-bugs'])],
 [mc('t712-a1', 'text-units', 'How many bytes does the word “भारत”, 4 code points of Devanagari, take in UTF-8?',
   [('a', '4 bytes'), ('b', '8 bytes'), ('c', '12 bytes'), ('d', '16 bytes')], 'c',
   'a: that counts code points, not bytes. b: that is the UTF-16 size, where each of these code points takes 2 bytes. c: correct, every Devanagari code point takes 3 bytes in UTF-8, so 4 × 3 = 12. d: 4 bytes per code point applies to emoji and other characters beyond U+FFFF, or to UTF-32.'),
  mc('t712-a2', 'normalisation', 'Two accounts named “José” exist although usernames are unique. They look identical and contain no spaces. What is the likely cause?',
   [('a', 'Two Unicode forms of “é”: one code point or two'), ('b', 'The unique index ignores the case of letters'),
    ('c', 'The two rows were written by different servers'), ('d', 'UTF-8 encodes the same “é” at random each time')], 'a',
   'a: correct, NFC “é” is U+00E9 while NFD is “e” plus U+0301, so the strings differ although they render the same; normalising at the edge fixes it. b: ignoring case would merge more names, not allow duplicates. c: the server that wrote a row does not change what a unique index compares. d: UTF-8 is deterministic; the same code points always give the same bytes.'),
  mc('t712-a3', 'identifiers', 'Usernames must be unique regardless of case. What should the unique index cover?',
   [('a', 'The username exactly as the user typed it'), ('b', 'A normalised, case-folded key of the name'),
    ('c', 'The lowercased name, without normalising'), ('d', 'A hash of the username as the user typed it')], 'b',
   'a: “Asha” and “asha” would both be accepted. b: correct, normalising then case-folding makes every spelling of the same name collide, while the typed form is kept for display. c: lowercasing misses names that differ only in Unicode form, and does not fold “ß” to “ss”. d: hashing the typed form keeps every difference the typed form had.'),
  mc('t712-a4', 'encoding-bugs', 'A customer’s name “José” appears as “JosÃ©” in an email the service sent. What happened?',
   [('a', 'UTF-8 bytes were decoded as Latin-1 somewhere'), ('b', 'The name was normalised to NFD before sending'),
    ('c', 'The database cut off the last character'), ('d', 'The email client’s font lacks a glyph for “é”')], 'a',
   'a: correct, “é” is two bytes in UTF-8 (C3 A9), and reading them as Latin-1 shows “Ã©”; this is called mojibake. b: NFD text renders the same as NFC. c: a cut would lose “é” or leave “�”, not add a character. d: a missing glyph shows as a box, not as two other letters.'),
  mc('t712-a5', 'encoding-bugs', 'A MySQL table using the legacy utf8 character set rejects a review containing “🎉”. Why?',
   [('a', 'That utf8 holds at most 3 bytes per character'), ('b', 'Emoji are not part of the Unicode standard'),
    ('c', 'The review must be normalised to NFC first'), ('d', 'Full-text indexes accept only ASCII text')], 'a',
   'a: correct, MySQL’s legacy utf8 is utf8mb3, and “🎉” needs 4 bytes; utf8mb4 stores it. b: emoji are Unicode code points like any other. c: normalisation does not change the emoji, which has no decomposition. d: full-text indexes in MySQL handle non-ASCII text; the column’s character set is the limit.'),
  order('t712-a6', 'normalisation', 'Order how a sign-up endpoint handles a username.',
   ['Decode the body as UTF-8, rejecting invalid bytes', 'Normalise the name to NFC',
    'Build the case-folded key and check lookalike rules', 'Check the length in visible characters',
    'Insert, relying on the unique index on the key'],
   ['s3', 's5', 's1', 's4', 's2'],
   'Text must be valid before it can be normalised, the key is built from the normalised form, limits are checked on the final text, and the database index is the last word on uniqueness.'),
  est('t712-a7', 'text-units', 'A display-name column allows 64 bytes. Names are written in Devanagari, which takes 3 bytes per code point in UTF-8. Roughly how many code points fit?', 'code points', 21,
   ['3 bytes per code point', 'No emoji or Latin letters mixed in'], '64 ÷ 3', '64 bytes ÷ 3 bytes per code point ≈ 21 code points',
   'Sanity check: a reader sees fewer than 21 characters, because vowel signs join the letter before them, so state limits in visible characters and size the byte column for the worst case.', 0.1, [64, 16])],
 flash(712, [('Bytes, code points, graphemes?', 'UTF-8 uses 1 to 4 bytes per code point; one visible character can be several code points.'),
             ('Why normalise text?', 'The same text can have different code points (“é” or “e” plus an accent); NFC makes equal text compare equal.'),
             ('Unique usernames?', 'Index a normalised, case-folded key, keep the typed form for display, and guard against lookalikes.')]),
 src([('RFC 3629: UTF-8, a transformation format of ISO 10646', 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3629'),
      ('W3C: Character Model for the World Wide Web: String Matching', 'https://www.w3.org/TR/charmod-norm/'),
      ('RFC 8265: Preparation, Enforcement and Comparison of Usernames and Passwords (PRECIS)', 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8265'),
      ('MySQL: The utf8mb4 character set', 'https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/charset-unicode-utf8mb4.html')]),
 deep(['text-units', 'normalisation', 'identifiers', 'encoding-bugs'], 'How text stays correct through a service',
  ["Bytes become text in one place. Request bodies are decoded as UTF-8, which RFC 8259 requires for JSON exchanged between systems, and invalid sequences are rejected with a 400 rather than replaced silently. Inside the service everything is text until it is encoded again at an edge: the database driver, a file, an outgoing request.",
   "Text is normalised at the edge to NFC so that equal-looking text has equal code points. NFKC goes further and folds compatibility characters, such as the full-width “Ａ” to “A” and the “ﬁ” ligature to “fi”; that suits identifiers and search keys, not text you will show back unchanged.",
   "Identifiers get a canonical key: normalise, case-fold, then apply identifier rules such as allowed scripts and no invisible characters outside the scripts that need them. The unique index and every lookup use the key; the typed form is kept only for display.",
   "Every limit names its unit. Storage and wire limits are in bytes; user-facing limits are usually grapheme clusters; database column lengths depend on the engine. Validate in the unit the user sees, make sure the byte budget fits the worst case of 4 bytes per code point, and truncate only on grapheme boundaries.",
   "UTF-8 is used end to end: database and connection character sets (utf8mb4 on MySQL), exported files with the encoding declared, and outgoing email with properly encoded headers. Sorting and case-insensitive comparison use a collation chosen on purpose, because alphabetical order differs between languages."],
  ['Sign-up, usernames, search, names and addresses: anything people type.', 'Services whose users write in more than one script.'],
  ['Avoid comparing or hashing user text before normalising it.', 'Avoid truncating by bytes or code points where a person can see the cut.'],
  ['Unit', 'Counts', 'Use it for'],
  [['Bytes (UTF-8)', '1 to 4 per code point', 'Storage, payload and protocol limits'],
   ['Code points', 'Unicode scalar values', 'Many databases’ VARCHAR(n)'],
   ['Grapheme clusters', 'What a reader sees as one character', 'User-facing limits and truncation']],
  'One name, three lengths',
  ['The Hindi name “निशा” (Nisha)', 'UTF-8 storage', 'Devanagari letters and vowel signs take 3 bytes each'],
  ['Code points: न, ि, श, ा = 4.',
   'Bytes: 4 × 3 = 12.',
   'Grapheme clusters: “नि” and “शा” = 2, which is what a reader counts.',
   'A 10-byte limit rejects this two-character name, while a 10-code-point limit counts it as 4 of 10.'],
  'Pick the unit for each limit on purpose, and size byte budgets for the worst case of 4 bytes per code point.',
  'Handles on a social app used across India', 'Illustrative example',
  "Illustrative: a social app lets people choose handles in English, Hindi and Tamil. Within weeks it found handles that looked identical but were distinct: “rahul” in Latin letters and “rаhul” with a Cyrillic “а”, and names pasted from documents in NFD form. The fix normalised every handle to NFC at the edge, stored a key made with NFKC and case folding under a unique index, allowed one script per handle plus digits, measured the 30-character limit in grapheme clusters, and sized the column for 4 bytes per code point.",
  [('“A string’s length is just its length.”', '“I’d name the unit: bytes for storage, grapheme clusters for what users see.”'),
   ('“Lowercase the username and put a unique index on it.”', '“I’d normalise, case-fold and check lookalikes, index that key, and keep the typed form for display.”')],
  ['UTF-8 uses 1 to 4 bytes per code point, and a visible character can span several code points.', 'Normalise to NFC at the edge before comparing, hashing or storing.',
   'Index a canonical, case-folded key for identifiers, and guard against lookalikes.', 'Name the unit of every limit and never cut text mid-character.']),
 {"kind": "predict", "title": "Truncating a Hindi name to 10 bytes", "prompt": "A service cuts display names to their first 10 bytes before saving them to a UTF-8 column. A user signs up as “निशा शर्मा”. What happens?",
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "The cut splits a character, leaving invalid UTF-8", "feedback": "Right: three code points take 9 bytes, so the tenth byte is the first of the fourth, and the row is rejected or saved with “�”."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "The name is saved as its first 10 letters", "feedback": "10 bytes hold at most 3 whole Devanagari code points, not 10 letters."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "Nothing goes wrong: byte cuts never split UTF-8", "feedback": "Most characters outside ASCII span several bytes, so a byte cut can land inside one."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "Measure the limit in the unit users see, truncate on grapheme cluster boundaries, and size byte columns for 4 bytes per code point."},
 see([(234, 'Decoding and normalising text is the first step in handling untrusted input'),
      (240, 'Serialisation formats fix the encoding text travels in; length and form are up to the service')])))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 713 mass assignment and over-exposed fields
T.append(topic(713, 'mass-assignment-and-response-shaping', R, 30, 'Request and Response Fields: Mass Assignment and Over-Exposure',
 ['Spot mass assignment, where a request sets fields its caller should not control',
  'Accept writes through explicit allowlists of fields per endpoint and role',
  'Return only the fields each caller may see, through explicit response shapes'],
 2, 12, [225],
 [text(713, 1, 'The model is not the API',
   "Many frameworks can copy a JSON body straight onto a database model, as in user.update(req.body). The model has fields its caller must never set: role, email_verified, balance, tenant_id, created_by. If the handler copies everything, a request carrying \"role\": \"admin\" makes the caller an admin. This is mass assignment, also called over-posting. OWASP’s API Security Top 10 (2023) groups it with over-exposed response fields under API3, broken object property level authorisation.",
   ['mass-assignment']),
  text(713, 2, 'Worked example: a profile update that grants admin',
   "PATCH /me accepts {\"name\": \"Asha\"} and the handler sets every key in the body as a column on the caller’s row. An attacker sends {\"name\": \"Asha\", \"role\": \"admin\", \"email_verified\": true}. Both extra keys are real columns, so the update succeeds. The fix is an input type for this endpoint holding only name, avatar and bio; any other field is rejected with a 400, and role changes go through a separate admin endpoint with its own authorisation check and audit record.",
   ['mass-assignment', 'write-allowlists']),
  diagram(713, 3, 'Explicit shapes in and out',
   'A request is parsed into the endpoint’s own type, and the response is built from another.', ['write-allowlists', 'response-shaping'],
   ['The body arrives: {"name": "Asha", "role": "admin"}',
    'Parse it into the endpoint’s input type: name, avatar and bio only',
    'The unknown field role is rejected with 400, or dropped and logged',
    'Check which allowed fields this caller may change, then update the model',
    'Map the model to the response type for this caller; never serialise it whole']),
  text(713, 4, 'Over-exposed responses',
   "The mirror-image bug is returning the whole row: the user object goes out with password_hash, mfa_secret and internal notes, and the app simply does not display them. Anyone can read the raw JSON. Define a response type per view: a public profile has name and avatar, the owner also sees their email, and an admin also sees the last sign-in address. Nested objects need the same care: an order list that embeds the full seller object leaks the seller’s phone number to every buyer.",
   ['response-shaping']),
  text(713, 5, 'Trap: a denylist of protected fields',
   "“Copy everything except role and is_admin” fails the day someone adds a column such as credit_limit or tenant_id, because the new field is writable by default. An allowlist fails closed: a new column is neither writable nor returned until someone adds it to an input or response type on purpose. Tests can lock this in by asserting each endpoint’s exact writable and returned fields for each role.",
   ['write-allowlists'])],
 [mc('t713-a1', 'mass-assignment', 'A sign-up handler runs User.create(req.body). The users table has email, password_hash, role and email_verified. What can an attacker do?',
   [('a', 'Sign up already verified, or as an admin'), ('b', 'Read the password hashes of other users'),
    ('c', 'Inject SQL through the email address'), ('d', 'Nothing, as the ORM checks column types')], 'a',
   'a: correct, any column named in the body is set, so "role": "admin" and "email_verified": true go straight in. b: that is over-exposure in responses, a different bug; this handler only writes. c: ORMs send values as parameters, so this is not an injection path. d: type checks pass happily, because "admin" is a valid string and true a valid boolean.'),
  mc('t713-a2', 'write-allowlists', 'A credit_limit column is added to users. Which design keeps it safe from client writes without anyone remembering to protect it?',
   [('a', 'A denylist of fields clients may not set'), ('b', 'An input type that lists the writable fields'),
    ('c', 'Leaving the field out of the web form'), ('d', 'A database trigger that logs every change')], 'b',
   'a: the denylist was written before the column existed, so the new field is writable by default. b: correct, a field becomes writable only when someone adds it to the input type on purpose. c: anyone can send fields the form does not show. d: a log records the abuse after it has happened instead of preventing it.'),
  mc('t713-a3', 'response-shaping', 'GET /users/42 returns the full user row, and the app shows only the name and avatar. What is the main problem?',
   [('a', 'None, because the app hides the other fields'), ('b', 'The response takes longer than it needs to'),
    ('c', 'Anyone can read the hidden fields in the JSON'), ('d', 'The client has to cache more than it needs')], 'c',
   'a: hiding a field in the interface is not access control. b: larger responses are slower, but that is a side effect. c: correct, the raw response can be read with any HTTP client or the browser’s network tab, password hash and all. d: extra caching is a minor cost next to leaking private fields.'),
  mc('t713-a4', 'response-shaping', 'A marketplace’s order list for buyers embeds each order’s seller object, serialised whole. What is most likely to leak?',
   [('a', 'The seller’s phone number and internal notes'), ('b', 'The buyer’s own delivery address'),
    ('c', 'Each order’s price and current status'), ('d', 'The seller’s public shop name and rating')], 'a',
   'a: correct, the whole seller row carries private contact details and staff notes that buyers should never see. b: the buyer is entitled to their own address. c: price and status are meant to be shown on the order. d: the shop name and rating are public by design.'),
  mc('t713-a5', 'write-allowlists', 'A PATCH body contains a field the endpoint does not accept. Which handling is the safest default?',
   [('a', 'Keep it in a JSON “extras” column for later'), ('b', 'Copy it onto the model if a column matches'),
    ('c', 'Reject with 400, or drop it and log it'), ('d', 'Accept it silently, in production only')], 'c',
   'a: storing unvetted fields invites someone to start trusting them later. b: that is mass assignment. c: correct, rejecting catches client bugs and probing early, and dropping with a log suits APIs that must tolerate newer clients; either way the field never reaches the model. d: behaving differently in production hides the problem where it matters most.'),
  order('t713-a6', 'write-allowlists', 'Order how an update endpoint handles a request body.',
   ['Parse the body into the endpoint’s input type', 'Reject or drop fields the type does not accept',
    'Check which accepted fields this caller may change', 'Apply those fields to the model and save it',
    'Map the result to the response type for this caller'],
   ['s2', 's4', 's1', 's5', 's3'],
   'Shape first, so nothing unexpected reaches later steps; authorise per field; write; then build the response from an explicit shape rather than the model.'),
  est('t713-a7', 'response-shaping', 'A list endpoint returns 50 users per page. A full serialised row is about 2 KB, and the public shape about 200 bytes. How many KB per page does returning the public shape save?', 'KB', 90,
   ['50 users per page', '2 KB per full row, 200 bytes per public shape', '1 KB = 1,000 bytes'], '50 × (2,000 − 200) bytes', '50 × 1,800 bytes = 90,000 bytes = 90 KB per page',
   'Sanity check: 100 KB falls to 10 KB, a 90% cut; at a million page views a day that is about 90 GB a day of data that should never have left the server.', 0.1, [100, 10])],
 flash(713, [('Mass assignment?', 'Copying a request body onto a model, so a caller can set fields like role or email_verified.'),
             ('Allowlist or denylist for writable fields?', 'Allowlist: a new column stays unwritable until someone adds it on purpose.'),
             ('How do you stop over-exposure?', 'A response type per view and role; never serialise the whole row.')]),
 src([('OWASP API Security Top 10 2023: API3 Broken Object Property Level Authorization', 'https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0xa3-broken-object-property-level-authorization/'),
      ('OWASP: Mass Assignment cheat sheet', 'https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Mass_Assignment_Cheat_Sheet.html'),
      ('CWE-915: Improperly Controlled Modification of Dynamically-Determined Object Attributes', 'https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/915.html')]),
 deep(['mass-assignment', 'write-allowlists', 'response-shaping'], 'How a handler decides which fields go in and out',
  ["Each endpoint defines an input type: the exact fields it accepts, with their types and limits. Parsing the body into that type is the first step, and nothing else from the body reaches the model. Create and update usually need different types, because fields like an email may be set once and changed only through a verified flow.",
   "Unknown fields are rejected with a 400 that names them, or dropped and logged; the choice is made once per API. Rejecting catches client bugs and probing early, while dropping makes it easier for older servers to accept newer clients. Either way, they are never copied.",
   "Field-level authorisation follows. Some fields are writable only by certain roles or in certain states, such as a listing’s price only by its seller and only before it is published. Sensitive changes, such as a role or an email address, go through dedicated endpoints with their own checks and audit records.",
   "The handler updates the model from the input type explicitly, field by field or through a mapping, never from the raw body. Server-owned fields, such as id, tenant_id, created_by, timestamps and balances, are set only by server code.",
   "On the way out, the model is mapped to a response type for the caller’s view: public, owner or admin. Lists and nested objects use the same shapes, so a seller embedded in an order uses the public seller shape. Tests assert the exact field sets per endpoint and role, so a new column cannot slip out unnoticed."],
  ['Any create or update endpoint backed by an ORM or a document store.', 'APIs whose responses are read by public or third-party clients.'],
  ['Avoid binding request bodies directly to database models.', 'Avoid denylists of forbidden fields, and returning whole rows.'],
  ['Approach', 'A new column, by default', 'Main risk'],
  [['Bind the whole body to the model', 'Writable', 'Any caller can change privileges or state'],
   ['Denylist of protected fields', 'Writable until someone remembers', 'A gap every time the schema grows'],
   ['Input and response types per endpoint', 'Neither writable nor returned', 'More types to maintain']],
  'What over-exposure costs on a list page',
  ['50 users per page', 'Full row serialised: about 2 KB each', 'Public shape: about 200 bytes each', '1 million page views a day'],
  ['Full rows: 50 × 2 KB = 100 KB per page.',
   'Public shape: 50 × 200 bytes = 10 KB per page.',
   'Saving: 90 KB per page, 90% of the payload.',
   'At 1 million page views a day: about 90 GB a day of data, some of it private, that never needed to leave the server.'],
  'Explicit response shapes are first a security control; smaller, faster responses are a welcome side effect.',
  'GitHub’s public-key form, 2012', 'Public incident, simplified',
  "In March 2012 a user exploited GitHub’s public-key update form by adding a user id field to the request (CVE-2012-2055). The server code passed every submitted attribute to the update, so his SSH key was attached to the account of a major open-source project’s organisation, and he used it to push a file to that project’s repository. GitHub’s post-mortem named the root cause as a failure to check incoming form parameters, the mass-assignment vulnerability; it rolled out a fix about an hour after the attack and audited its codebase for other instances. The web framework involved later made an explicit allowlist of permitted fields the standard way to accept input.",
  [('“The front end only sends the fields in the form.”', '“Anyone can send any JSON, so I’d parse into an input type that accepts only the writable fields.”'),
   ('“The client ignores fields it doesn’t display.”', '“The raw JSON is readable by anyone, so I’d return a response shape per role.”')],
  ['Never bind a request body straight onto a model.', 'Allowlist writable fields per endpoint and role; reject or drop the rest.',
   'Return a response type per view, never the whole row.', 'Test the exact field sets, so new columns stay private by default.']),
 {"kind": "predict", "title": "A new column meets a denylist", "prompt": "A handler updates users with every key in the request body except role and is_admin. A developer adds a credit_limit column. What happens next?",
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "Any user can raise their own credit limit", "feedback": "Right: the denylist was written before credit_limit existed, so the new column is writable by default."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "Updates fail until the field is listed somewhere", "feedback": "That is how an allowlist behaves; a denylist lets new fields straight through."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "Only admins can change it, as with role", "feedback": "Nothing ties credit_limit to the role check; only the two named fields are blocked."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "Allowlists fail closed. Write an input type for each endpoint, and add a field to it only when that caller should control it."},
 see([(234, 'Untrusted input: validating what arrives before it reaches the model'),
      (225, 'Field-level checks are authorisation applied to individual properties'),
      (231, 'Explicit response types mean a new column never reaches the API by accident')])))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 535 long-running operations
T.append(topic(535, 'long-running-operations-in-apis', R, 31, 'Long-Running Operations in APIs',
 ['Accept slow work with 202 Accepted and a status resource the client can poll',
  'Keep polling cheap and retries safe with Retry-After, callbacks and idempotency keys',
  'Support cancellation, failure states and expiry of operations and their results'],
 2, 12, [227],
 [text(535, 1, 'Hand back a ticket, not a held connection',
   "Generating a 2 GB export can take four minutes. Holding the HTTP request open that long fails in ordinary ways: proxies and load balancers close idle connections (60 seconds is a common default), mobile networks drop, and every waiting request holds a server connection. Instead, accept the work, queue it and reply 202 Accepted with a Location header such as /operations/op_81. The client polls that URL until the operation finishes, then fetches the result.",
   ['async-202']),
  diagram(535, 2, 'Life of an operation',
   'The status resource is the record of where the work stands.', ['async-202', 'operation-states'],
   ['POST /exports with an Idempotency-Key; reply 202 and Location: /operations/op_81',
    'A worker picks it up: status moves from queued to running, with progress',
    'The client polls the status URL, waiting as long as Retry-After says',
    'Finished: status done with a link to the result, or failed with an error',
    'The operation and its result expire after a stated time, such as 7 days']),
  text(535, 3, 'Worked example: what polling costs',
   "50,000 exports a day, each running about 4 minutes. If clients poll every second, that is 240 polls each, or 12M status requests a day for 50,000 jobs. Polling every 10 seconds cuts it to 1.2M. Backing off instead (1, 2, 4, 8, 16 s, then every 30 s) needs only about a dozen polls per job, around 600,000 a day. Send Retry-After on each status reply so the server, not the client, sets the pace.",
   ['polling']),
  text(535, 4, 'Callbacks alongside the status URL',
   "A webhook on completion saves clients from polling at all, but webhooks get lost: the receiver is down, a firewall blocks it, or a retry gives up. Keep the status URL as the record and treat the callback as a hint that it is worth checking now. Sign callbacks so the receiver can tell they came from you, and include only the operation id, so a forged or replayed callback reveals nothing.",
   ['polling']),
  text(535, 5, 'Trap: a retried POST starts a second job',
   "The client’s POST times out before the 202 arrives, so it retries, and two exports run. Require an Idempotency-Key on the POST and return the same operation id for a repeat. Also plan for the unhappy endings: a cancel request moves the operation to cancelling and then cancelled, workers check for it between chunks, a worker that stops sending heartbeats gets its operation retried or marked failed, and done, failed and cancelled are final.",
   ['async-idempotency', 'operation-states'])],
 [mc('t535-a1', 'async-202', 'An endpoint starts a report that takes about 3 minutes to build. What should the first response be?',
   [('a', '200 OK, sent once the report finishes'), ('b', '202 Accepted with a status URL'),
    ('c', '201 Created with an empty report body'), ('d', '504 Gateway Timeout, asking for a retry')], 'b',
   'a: the connection must stay open for 3 minutes, and proxies may close it first. b: correct, the work is accepted and the client gets somewhere to check on it. c: 201 says the resource exists now, which it does not. d: a 504 tells the client something failed, and a retry would start the work again.'),
  mc('t535-a2', 'polling', 'How should a server tell clients how often to poll an operation’s status?',
   [('a', 'Close the connection when a client polls too soon'), ('b', 'Reply 429 to every poll until the work is done'),
    ('c', 'Document a fixed 1-second interval for all'), ('d', 'Send a Retry-After header on each status reply')], 'd',
   'a: closing connections looks like a network error and invites fast retries. b: 429 to well-behaved clients turns normal polling into errors. c: a fixed 1-second interval creates heavy load for long jobs. d: correct, the server can lengthen the wait for long jobs or under load.'),
  mc('t535-a3', 'operation-states', 'A status URL says “running” for hours because the worker crashed. What fixes this?',
   [('a', 'Clients poll more often so they notice sooner'), ('b', 'A heartbeat timeout that retries or marks it failed'),
    ('c', 'The status endpoint returns 500 while a job runs'), ('d', 'Status records are deleted after one hour')], 'b',
   'a: polling faster just reads the same stale status more often. b: correct, a worker that stops heartbeating has its operation retried or moved to failed, so clients get a final state. c: a 500 tells the client the status service broke, not the job. d: deleting the record leaves the client with a 404 and no explanation.'),
  mc('t535-a4', 'async-idempotency', 'A POST /exports times out before the 202 arrives, and the client retries with the same Idempotency-Key. What should happen?',
   [('a', 'A second export starts under a new id'), ('b', 'The server replies 409 with no id'),
    ('c', 'The server returns the same operation id'), ('d', 'The first export is cancelled and restarted')], 'c',
   'a: that is the duplicate the key exists to prevent. b: a 409 leaves the client unable to find the job it started. c: correct, the client gets the status URL of the export that is already running. d: restarting wastes the work already done.'),
  mc('t535-a5', 'polling', 'Why keep a status URL when you also send a completion webhook?',
   [('a', 'Webhooks cannot carry the result’s location'), ('b', 'Status URLs are needed to authenticate'),
    ('c', 'Clients must poll before a webhook is sent'), ('d', 'Webhooks can be lost; the URL is the record')], 'd',
   'a: a webhook can carry a link, but it may never arrive. b: authentication is separate from how status is reported. c: clients that get webhooks need not poll at all on the happy path. d: correct, a client that missed the callback can still find out the result.'),
  order('t535-a6', 'operation-states', 'Order a long-running export from the client’s side.',
   ['POST with an Idempotency-Key', 'Receive 202 and the status URL', 'Poll, waiting as long as Retry-After says',
    'See status done with a result link', 'Download the result before it expires'],
   ['s4', 's1', 's5', 's3', 's2'],
   'The key makes the first step safe to retry, and the expiry on the result is why the last step has a deadline.'),
  est('t535-a7', 'polling', '30,000 operations a day each run about 5 minutes, and clients poll every 2 seconds. Roughly how many status polls is that per day?', 'polls/day', 4500000,
   ['Each operation is polled from start to finish', '5 minutes is 300 seconds'], '30,000 × 300 ÷ 2', '150 polls each × 30,000 = 4.5M polls a day',
   'Design implication: a Retry-After of 30 s cuts this to 300,000 polls a day, and a completion webhook cuts it to almost none for clients that can receive one.', 0.1, [30000, 450000])],
 flash(535, [('First response to slow work?', '202 Accepted with a Location header pointing at a status resource.'),
             ('How is polling kept cheap?', 'Retry-After on each status reply, client backoff, and optional webhooks.'),
             ('Final states of an operation?', 'done (with a result link), failed (with an error) or cancelled.')]),
 src([('RFC 9110: 202 Accepted', 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#name-202-accepted'),
      ('RFC 9110: Retry-After', 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#name-retry-after'),
      ('Microsoft Learn: Asynchronous Request-Reply pattern', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/async-request-reply')]),
 deep(['async-202', 'operation-states', 'polling', 'async-idempotency'], 'How an API runs work that outlasts a request',
  ["The client sends the request with an Idempotency-Key. The server validates it, checks the key and stores a new operation record, with an id, a status of queued, the request parameters and an expiry time, in the same transaction that enqueues the job. It replies 202 Accepted, a Location header pointing at /operations/{id}, and a small body with the id and status. If the key was seen before, it returns the existing operation instead.",
   "A worker takes the job, marks the operation running and updates progress as it goes, such as rows exported or a percentage. It sends heartbeats while it works. A supervisor looks for running operations whose heartbeat has stopped, then retries them if the work is idempotent or marks them failed with a reason, so no client polls a dead job forever.",
   "Clients poll the status URL. Each reply says the status and carries a Retry-After header, short at first and longer for jobs known to be slow or when the system is busy. Clients back off between polls and stop at a final state. Clients that can receive webhooks register a callback URL and get a signed notification on completion, but the status resource remains the record they can always check.",
   "The operation ends in one final state. done includes a link to the result, often a short-lived signed URL to a file in object storage. failed includes a machine-readable error code and a message. cancelled follows a cancel request: the API marks the operation cancelling, the worker checks for that between chunks, cleans up and marks it cancelled. A final state never changes again.",
   "Operations and results do not live forever. The API documents how long they are kept, for example 7 days, after which the status URL returns 404 or 410 and the result file is deleted. A cleanup job enforces this. The documented limit lets clients plan, and it stops storage from growing without bound as millions of old exports pile up."],
  ['Exports, reports, media processing and other work that takes more than a few seconds.', 'Requests whose duration varies widely and can exceed proxy timeouts.'],
  ['Avoid it for work that reliably finishes within about a second; a direct reply is simpler.', 'Avoid polling every second with no Retry-After; it creates load out of nothing.'],
  ['Pattern', 'Client effort', 'Server cost', 'When things go wrong'],
  [['Synchronous reply', 'None', 'A held connection per request', 'Timeouts lose the result'],
   ['202 plus polling', 'Poll and back off', 'Status reads', 'Client can always check status'],
   ['202 plus webhook', 'Receive callbacks', 'Delivery and retries', 'Lost callbacks need a fallback'],
   ['202, polling and webhook', 'Callback, with polling as backup', 'Both', 'Most robust']],
  'Held connections versus a ticket',
  ['1 export request arrives per second', 'Each export takes 240 s to build', 'The 202 reply takes about 50 ms'],
  ['Synchronous: by Little’s law, concurrent requests = arrival rate × time = 1 × 240 = 240 connections held open.',
   'Each held connection also needs a worker waiting on it, and any proxy with a 60 s idle timeout will cut them first.',
   'Asynchronous: 1 × 0.05 s = 0.05 connections busy on average for the requests themselves.',
   'The 240 builds still run, but on queue workers whose number you choose, not on request threads.'],
  'A ticket moves long work off the request path, where it no longer holds connections or depends on proxy timeouts.',
  'Bank statements and tax data exports', 'Illustrative example',
  "Illustrative: an accounting app lets a firm download twelve months of transactions for all its clients ahead of the tax filing deadline. The export can take several minutes and many firms ask in the same week. The API returns 202 with a status URL, sends Retry-After of 15 seconds and then longer, and notifies the firm’s webhook when the file is ready. The result link expires after seven days, and a retried request with the same idempotency key returns the export already running instead of starting another.",
  [('“We just raised the load balancer timeout to 10 minutes.”', '“I’d return 202 with a status URL, so long work does not depend on any proxy timeout.”'),
   ('“The webhook tells them when it is done.”', '“I’d keep a status URL as the record too, because callbacks can be lost.”')],
  ['Reply 202 with a status URL for work that outlasts a request.', 'Use Retry-After and backoff to keep polling cheap; add signed callbacks.',
   'Require an idempotency key so a retried POST does not start a second job.', 'Define final states, heartbeats, cancellation and expiry.']),
 {"kind": "predict", "title": "A slow PDF behind a load balancer", "prompt": "An endpoint builds PDFs synchronously. Most take 20 s, but large ones take 90 s. The load balancer closes connections idle for 60 s. What do users with large PDFs see?",
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "A timeout error while the server keeps building the PDF", "feedback": "Right: the load balancer gives up at 60 s, but the work continues and its result has nowhere to go."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "The PDF, just after a longer wait", "feedback": "The connection is closed at 60 s, before the reply exists."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "A 202 response that the load balancer adds for them", "feedback": "Load balancers do not convert slow requests into asynchronous ones."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "The user gets an error, retries and starts a second 90-second build. Return 202 with a status URL, build in a worker, and the timeout stops mattering."},
 see([(227, 'The work behind a 202 runs as a background job on a queue'),
      (224, 'Idempotency keys make the starting POST safe to retry'),
      (237, 'Completion callbacks are webhooks and should be signed and retried the same way'),
      (537, 'Account deletion across services is a long-running operation with a status')])))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 536 testing a service
T.append(topic(536, 'testing-a-backend-service', R, 32, 'Testing a Service: Unit, Integration and Contract Tests',
 ['Match unit, integration, contract and end-to-end tests to the risks they catch',
  'Test data access against a real database instead of a mock that agrees with you',
  'Keep tests fast and repeatable by controlling time, randomness and shared state'],
 2, 13, [221],
 [text(536, 1, 'Match the test to the risk',
   "Unit tests check pure logic, such as a price or tax calculation, in milliseconds. Integration tests run your code against real dependencies, above all the database, and catch wrong SQL, missing migrations and violated constraints, in about a second each. Contract tests check that your API still gives each consumer the fields and types it relies on. End-to-end tests drive a whole user journey across services; they are slow and fail for unrelated reasons, so keep a few for the critical paths.",
   ['test-levels']),
  text(536, 2, 'Worked example: the bug a mock agreed with',
   "A sign-up handler relies on a unique constraint on email and turns the database’s unique-violation error into a 409. The unit test mocks the repository to “throw a duplicate error”, and passes. In production the driver reports the violation with a different error code from the one the handler checks, so users get a 500. Only a test against a real database, started fresh in a container and migrated with the real migrations, exercises the constraint and the actual error the driver returns.",
   ['real-dependencies']),
  diagram(536, 3, 'A consumer-driven contract test',
   'Each consumer states what it relies on; the provider checks it on every build.', ['contract-tests'],
   ['The consumer records the requests it sends and the response fields it reads',
    'Those expectations are published as a contract file',
    'The provider’s build replays each request against the real provider',
    'A removed field or changed type fails the provider’s build, before release',
    'Both sides deploy knowing they still agree']),
  text(536, 4, 'Flaky tests come from hidden inputs',
   "A test that sometimes fails usually reads something it does not control: the clock, random numbers, test order, data left by another test, or a real network call. Pass in a clock the test can set, seed random generators, give each test its own data (a rolled-back transaction or unique ids), and wait for a condition rather than a fixed sleep. Flakiness adds up: 400 tests that each fail 0.5% of the time make 1 − 0.995^400 ≈ 86% of runs go red.",
   ['flaky-tests']),
  text(536, 5, 'Trap: chasing coverage instead of risks',
   "90% line coverage can still miss the concurrent double booking, the migration that locks a table, the timeout path and the retried request. Coverage shows which lines ran, not whether the failure cases were checked. List the ways the service can fail, such as a dependency timeout, a duplicate request, a partial write or a bad input, and write a test for each one.",
   ['test-levels'])],
 [mc('t536-a1', 'test-levels', 'Which test best catches a wrong tax calculation for a cart?',
   [('a', 'An end-to-end test run through the browser'), ('b', 'A contract test against the payments API'),
    ('c', 'A unit test of the price function'), ('d', 'A load test at expected peak traffic')], 'c',
   'a: it could catch it, slowly, and a failure would not say which part was wrong. b: a contract checks the shape of calls between services, not the arithmetic. c: correct, pure logic is best tested directly with many cases, including rounding edges. d: load tests measure capacity, not correctness.'),
  mc('t536-a2', 'real-dependencies', 'A test with a mocked database passes, but production returns 500 on duplicate sign-ups. What would have caught it?',
   [('a', 'More unit tests around the mocked handler'), ('b', 'A higher line-coverage threshold in CI'),
    ('c', 'Running the same suite twice in a row'), ('d', 'An integration test on a real database')], 'd',
   'a: more tests against the same mock repeat the same wrong assumption. b: the lines already ran; coverage cannot see the wrong error mapping. c: a deterministic test passes twice just as easily. d: correct, a real database raises the real constraint error and exposes the mapping bug.'),
  mc('t536-a3', 'contract-tests', 'A provider team renames a JSON field. With consumer-driven contract tests, what fails first?',
   [('a', 'The provider’s own build'), ('b', 'The consumer’s production traffic'),
    ('c', 'The consumer’s unit tests'), ('d', 'A nightly end-to-end run')], 'a',
   'a: correct, the provider replays each consumer’s expectations and the missing field fails its build before release. b: that is what happens without contracts. c: the consumer’s tests use its own stub of the provider, which still has the old field. d: an end-to-end run might notice later, if it covers that field.'),
  mc('t536-a4', 'flaky-tests', 'A test checks that a token “expires in 1 hour” and fails a few times a week, always near midnight. What is the right fix?',
   [('a', 'Retry the test up to three times in CI'), ('b', 'Inject a clock the test can control'),
    ('c', 'Raise the test’s timeout to one hour'), ('d', 'Run the suite only during working hours')], 'b',
   'a: retries hide the flakiness and the bug it may point to. b: correct, the code reads time from a clock the test sets, so the result no longer depends on when it runs. c: the failure is about dates, not slowness. d: avoiding midnight hides a real date-boundary bug that users will hit.'),
  mc('t536-a5', 'flaky-tests', 'Tests pass one at a time but fail when the suite runs in random order. What is the likely cause?',
   [('a', 'The CI machine has too little memory'), ('b', 'A race inside the test framework itself'),
    ('c', 'Shared state left behind by another test'), ('d', 'The database is too slow under parallel load')], 'c',
   'a: memory pressure fails tests regardless of order. b: possible but rare; suspect your own tests first. c: correct, one test leaves data or settings that another depends on or trips over; isolate each test’s data. d: slowness causes timeouts, which do not depend on order.'),
  order('t536-a6', 'real-dependencies', 'Order the steps of an isolated database integration test.',
   ['Start a fresh database, for example in a container', 'Apply the real migrations', 'Begin a transaction for this test',
    'Run the code and check the rows it wrote', 'Roll back so the next test starts clean'],
   ['s3', 's5', 's1', 's4', 's2'],
   'Real migrations prove the schema, and the rollback gives every test the same starting point without rebuilding the database.'),
  est('t536-a7', 'flaky-tests', 'A suite has 300 tests and each fails at random 0.2% of the time, independently. Roughly what percent of runs fail at least once?', '%', 45,
   ['Failures are independent', 'No real bugs are present'], '1 − 0.998^300', '0.998^300 ≈ 0.55, so about 45% of runs',
   'Sanity check: nearly half the runs go red with no real bug, so people stop trusting the suite; each flaky test is worth fixing.', 0.1, [0.2, 60])],
 flash(536, [('What does an integration test catch that a unit test misses?', 'Wrong SQL, missing migrations and real constraint errors from the actual database.'),
             ('Consumer-driven contract?', 'Consumers publish what they rely on; the provider’s build checks it before release.'),
             ('Usual causes of flaky tests?', 'Time, randomness, test order, shared data and real network calls.')]),
 src([('Martin Fowler: The Practical Test Pyramid', 'https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html'),
      ('Martin Fowler: Consumer-Driven Contracts', 'https://martinfowler.com/articles/consumerDrivenContracts.html'),
      ('Martin Fowler: Eradicating Non-Determinism in Tests', 'https://martinfowler.com/articles/nonDeterminism.html')]),
 deep(['test-levels', 'real-dependencies', 'contract-tests', 'flaky-tests'], 'How a backend test suite is put together',
  ["Start from risks, not layers. List what can go wrong: business rules computed wrongly, queries that return the wrong rows, a migration that breaks old data, an API change that breaks a consumer, a dependency that times out, a request that is retried. Each risk points at the cheapest kind of test that can catch it, and that kind is the one to write.",
   "Pure logic gets unit tests: functions that take values and return values, with no database or network. Keep business rules in such functions where you can, because they can then be tested with hundreds of cases, including rounding edges and empty inputs, in well under a second. Mocks belong at the boundary of this code, and only for things that are slow or outside your control.",
   "Data access gets integration tests against the real database engine, the same version as production, started fresh for the test run, often in a container, and built with the real migrations. Each test runs inside a transaction that is rolled back, or uses its own unique ids, so tests do not see each other’s data. These tests catch the bugs mocks cannot: SQL errors, constraint errors, wrong isolation assumptions and slow queries.",
   "Service boundaries get contract tests. Each consumer records the requests it makes and the parts of the response it reads; the provider’s pipeline replays them on every build. A provider can then remove a field nobody reads without fear, and is stopped from removing one that someone does. A small number of end-to-end tests cover the journeys that would cost the most to break, such as sign-up and checkout.",
   "Everything runs on every change, so speed and determinism matter. Time, randomness and identifiers are passed in so tests can fix them. Waits poll for a condition with a timeout instead of sleeping. Tests that turn out to be flaky are quarantined and fixed quickly, because a suite that fails at random teaches people to ignore red builds, and then real failures get ignored too."],
  ['Any service that will be changed by more than one person over time.', 'Services with consumers in other teams, where contracts prevent surprise breakage.'],
  ['Avoid mocking the database to test queries; the mock agrees with whatever you assumed.', 'Avoid a large end-to-end suite as the main safety net; it is slow and fails for unrelated reasons.'],
  ['Test kind', 'Catches', 'Speed', 'Main cost'],
  [['Unit', 'Logic errors', 'Milliseconds', 'Misses integration bugs'],
   ['Integration with a real database', 'SQL, migrations, constraints', 'Around a second each', 'Needs a database per run'],
   ['Contract', 'Breaking API changes', 'Seconds', 'Consumers must maintain contracts'],
   ['End-to-end', 'Whole-journey breaks', 'Tens of seconds each', 'Slow and flaky']],
  'A CI budget of 10 minutes',
  ['2,000 unit tests at about 5 ms each', '200 integration tests at about 300 ms each', '20 end-to-end tests at about 30 s each', '4 parallel CI workers'],
  ['Unit: 2,000 × 5 ms = 10 s.',
   'Integration: 200 × 0.3 s = 60 s.',
   'End-to-end: 20 × 30 s = 600 s, ten times the other two combined.',
   'Total 670 s, about 11 minutes on one worker; split over 4 workers, about 2.8 minutes.'],
  'End-to-end tests dominate the time even when there are few of them. Keep each one for a journey that really needs it, and push the rest down to integration and unit tests.',
  'A payments integration tested before go-live', 'Illustrative example',
  "Illustrative: a team adding UPI payments to an app tests its fee and refund arithmetic with unit tests, runs integration tests against a real database to check that a repeated payment callback is rejected by a unique constraint, and runs contract tests against the payment provider’s published sandbox behaviour. A handful of end-to-end tests cover pay, refund and a timed-out payment. The flakiest test turned out to depend on the time of day and was fixed by passing in a clock.",
  [('“We have 90% coverage, so it is well tested.”', '“Coverage shows which lines ran; I’d check that each failure mode, like timeouts and retries, has a test.”'),
   ('“We mock the database so tests are fast.”', '“I’d mock it in unit tests but run data access against a real database, since that is where the bugs are.”')],
  ['Pick the cheapest test that can catch each risk.', 'Test queries and migrations against a real database.',
   'Contract tests stop breaking API changes before release.', 'Control time, randomness and shared data to keep tests repeatable.']),
 {"kind": "tradeoff", "title": "A test plan for an orders service", "prompt": "Pick the best fit for these constraints.",
  "setup": ['The orders service owns a relational database schema', 'Three other teams call its API', 'CI must finish in under 10 minutes'],
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "Unit tests, real-database integration tests and consumer contracts", "feedback": "Right: each risk is covered by the cheapest test that catches it, inside the time budget."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "A large end-to-end suite that runs all four services", "feedback": "Catches cross-service breaks, but it is slow, fails for unrelated reasons and will not fit 10 minutes."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "Unit tests with a mocked database, for speed", "feedback": "Fast, but SQL, migrations and constraints go untested, and API changes reach consumers unchecked."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "Logic in unit tests, data access against a real database, and contracts for the three consumers cover the main risks in a few minutes. Add a handful of end-to-end tests for the journeys that would cost most to break."},
 see([(323, 'These tests are the gates a CI/CD pipeline runs on every change'),
      (231, 'Contract tests are how you know an API change is still backward compatible'),
      (240, 'Schema compatibility checks are contract tests for messages and events')])))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 537 deleting data
T.append(topic(537, 'deleting-data-and-retention', R, 33, 'Deleting Data Across a Service: Cascades, Tombstones and Workflows',
 ['Choose the ON DELETE action for each foreign key, and stage large deletes instead of one huge cascade',
  'Run account deletion as a tracked workflow across services, with idempotent steps',
  'Stop deleted data coming back through retries, replays, caches and offline sync'],
 2, 13, [238],
 [text(537, 1, 'What a foreign key does on delete',
   "ON DELETE CASCADE removes child rows with the parent; RESTRICT (or NO ACTION) refuses the delete while children exist; SET NULL keeps the child and clears the link. Choose per relationship: an order’s line items cascade with the order, a customer’s invoices restrict the delete until they are dealt with, and a comment’s author can become null (“deleted user”). Index every foreign-key column: PostgreSQL does not create that index for you, and without it each parent delete scans the whole child table.",
   ['fk-actions']),
  text(537, 2, 'Worked example: deleting a workspace with 3 million rows',
   "DELETE FROM workspaces WHERE id = 42, with cascades to projects, tasks, comments and attachments, is one statement but one transaction over 3 million rows: locks held for minutes, a surge of write-ahead log, replicas falling behind, and if it times out, everything rolls back and starts again. Instead mark the workspace deleting, so the app hides it and refuses writes, then delete the children leaf-first in batches of a few thousand rows (comments, tasks, projects), and the workspace row last, when the cascades have nothing left to do. Files in object storage are outside the database, so the same job deletes them by prefix.",
   ['fk-actions', 'deletion-workflow']),
  diagram(537, 3, 'Account deletion as a workflow',
   'Deletion has an id and a status, and it is finished only when every owner says so.', ['deletion-workflow'],
   ['Request accepted: the account is marked deleting and sign-in is blocked',
    'A UserDeleted event is written to the outbox in the same transaction',
    'Each service that owns data deletes or anonymises it and acknowledges',
    'The workflow tracks each service’s status and retries failed steps',
    'When all have acknowledged, the account becomes a tombstone and completion is recorded']),
  text(537, 4, 'Tombstones stop data coming back',
   "Deleted data returns in quiet ways: a retried job re-creates a profile from a queued message, a cache refills from a lagging replica, an offline phone syncs its old copy, a replayed event rebuilds a read model. Keep a small tombstone, the id and when it was deleted with no personal data, and make writers, consumers and sync endpoints check it: an upsert for a tombstoned id is dropped, and sync answers with a delete marker. Keep tombstones for longer than any queue, cache or offline client can hold an old copy.",
   ['tombstones']),
  text(537, 5, 'Trap: “deleted” after one service says so',
   "If the API returns success as soon as it has deleted its own rows and fired an untracked event, a consumer that was down or has a bug leaves data behind, and nobody finds out. Treat deletion as a long-running operation: give it an id and a status, require every service that owns data to acknowledge, retry failed steps, and alert on any step stuck past the promised window. Each step must be idempotent, so deleting something already gone counts as success.",
   ['deletion-workflow'])],
 [mc('t537-a1', 'fk-actions', 'Invoices reference customers, and accounting rules say invoices must be kept. Which ON DELETE action fits that foreign key?',
   [('a', 'CASCADE, so invoices go with the customer'), ('b', 'RESTRICT, so the delete waits for a decision'),
    ('c', 'SET NULL, leaving invoices with no customer'), ('d', 'No foreign key, so nothing ever blocks')], 'b',
   'a: a cascade silently destroys records the business must keep. b: correct, the delete fails until code deals with the invoices on purpose, for example by anonymising the customer’s personal fields while keeping the invoice. c: invoices lose who was billed, which accounting still needs, and it happens without anyone deciding. d: without the constraint, invoices can point at customers that no longer exist.'),
  mc('t537-a2', 'fk-actions', 'In PostgreSQL, deleting one customer takes 40 seconds, though they have only 12 orders. orders.customer_id references customers with ON DELETE CASCADE. What is the likely cause?',
   [('a', 'orders.customer_id has no index, so orders is scanned'), ('b', 'CASCADE locks the entire orders table'),
    ('c', 'The customers table needs a VACUUM first'), ('d', 'Foreign keys are only checked at commit time')], 'a',
   'a: correct, PostgreSQL indexes the referenced key but not the referencing column, so finding the 12 orders means reading the whole table. b: a cascade locks the child rows it deletes, not the table. c: dead rows can slow scans a little, but not into tens of seconds for one customer. d: when the check runs does not change how the child rows are found.'),
  mc('t537-a3', 'deletion-workflow', 'Deleting a workspace cascades to 3 million rows in one statement and keeps timing out. What is the better approach?',
   [('a', 'Mark it deleting, then delete its children in batches'), ('b', 'Run the same statement at night with no timeout'),
    ('c', 'Drop the foreign keys so the delete runs instantly'), ('d', 'Soft-delete the workspace and keep every row')], 'a',
   'a: correct, short batch transactions keep locks brief and replicas close, the job can resume after a failure, and the deleting flag hides the workspace meanwhile. b: at night it still holds locks for minutes, floods replicas, and restarts from zero on any failure. c: the rows still have to be deleted, and the database stops protecting the links. d: hiding is not deleting, and the rows keep growing.'),
  mc('t537-a4', 'tombstones', 'A user was deleted, yet their profile reappears a day later after a queued “update profile” message was retried. What prevents this?',
   [('a', 'A tombstone that consumers check before writing'), ('b', 'A longer delay between retries on the queue'),
    ('c', 'Running the deletion twice to make sure'), ('d', 'Clearing the cache after every deletion')], 'a',
   'a: correct, the consumer sees that the id was deleted and drops the upsert. b: the message still arrives eventually, whatever the delay. c: the late message can arrive after the second run too. d: the profile came back through a database write, not the cache.'),
  mc('t537-a5', 'deletion-workflow', 'Account deletion publishes UserDeleted and the API reports “deleted” at once. The search service was down for an hour that day. What is the gap?',
   [('a', 'Nothing records whether every service finished'), ('b', 'The event should have been sent as an email'),
    ('c', 'UserDeleted events can never be retried'), ('d', 'The API should not publish any events at all')], 'a',
   'a: correct, without a status per service nobody knows the search index still holds the user, so nothing retries or alerts. b: the delivery channel is not the problem; tracking is. c: events can be redelivered, but only if something notices the step failed. d: an event is a fine way to tell services, as long as their acknowledgements are tracked.'),
  order('t537-a6', 'deletion-workflow', 'Order how a service handles an account deletion across services.',
   ['Mark the account deleting and block sign-in', 'Write UserDeleted to the outbox in the same transaction',
    'Each owning service deletes or anonymises its data and acknowledges', 'Retry and alert on any step that has not acknowledged',
    'Turn the account into a tombstone and record completion'],
   ['s3', 's5', 's1', 's4', 's2'],
   'Stop new activity first, publish reliably with the state change, collect acknowledgements, chase the stragglers, and only then declare the deletion complete.'),
  est('t537-a7', 'fk-actions', 'A job deletes a workspace’s 3 million child rows in batches of 2,000. Each batch takes 0.1 s, plus a 0.1 s pause before the next. How many minutes does it take?', 'minutes', 5,
   ['Batch time stays steady', 'No other load on the table'], '3,000,000 ÷ 2,000 × 0.2 s ÷ 60', '1,500 batches × 0.2 s = 300 s = 5 minutes',
   'Sanity check: 5 minutes of short transactions is harmless, while one 3-million-row statement would hold its locks for the whole time and roll everything back on failure.', 0.1, [0.5, 50])],
 flash(537, [('CASCADE, RESTRICT or SET NULL?', 'Cascade parts of a thing, restrict records that must be kept, set null for links that may outlive the parent.'),
             ('Why keep a tombstone?', 'So retries, replays, caches and offline sync cannot bring deleted data back.'),
             ('Deletion across services?', 'A tracked workflow: event through the outbox, an acknowledgement per service, retries and alerts until all are done.')]),
 src([('PostgreSQL: Constraints (foreign keys)', 'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html'),
      ('Microsoft Azure Architecture Center: Saga pattern', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/saga'),
      ('OWASP: User Privacy Protection cheat sheet', 'https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/User_Privacy_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html')]),
 deep(['fk-actions', 'deletion-workflow', 'tombstones'], 'How a deletion travels through a service and its neighbours',
  ["Every foreign key gets an explicit ON DELETE chosen by ownership: parts of a thing cascade, records with their own duties restrict, optional links set null. Every referencing column is indexed, so cascades and checks find children without scanning.",
   "Large deletes are staged. The parent is marked deleting, hidden and read-only; a batch job deletes children leaf-first in short transactions with a checkpoint; the parent goes last. The same job removes what the database does not hold, such as files in object storage, keyed or prefixed by the parent’s id.",
   "Deletion across services is a workflow with an id and a status. The owning service commits the deleting state and an outbox event in one transaction; each consumer deletes or anonymises idempotently and acknowledges; the coordinator retries, alerts when a step exceeds the promised window, and records completion.",
   "Tombstones hold the id and deletion time, without personal data, for as long as any queue, cache, replica or offline client could still carry an old copy. Writers check them, sync endpoints return delete markers, and event consumers treat a tombstoned id as final.",
   "Exceptions are designed in, not bolted on. Records that a law or contract requires you to keep, often invoices and payment records, are anonymised rather than deleted; a legal hold pauses a deletion; and the completion record lists what was kept and why, without the deleted data itself. Which records must be kept, and for how long, depends on the jurisdiction, so the rules come from legal advice, not from engineering."],
  ['Account and workspace deletion, tenant offboarding, and removing objects with many children.', 'Systems where several services, caches or devices hold copies of the same records.'],
  ['Avoid one cascading DELETE over millions of rows in a live transaction.', 'Avoid fire-and-forget deletion events with no acknowledgement or status.'],
  ['ON DELETE', 'Effect on child rows', 'Fits'],
  [['CASCADE', 'Deleted with the parent', 'Parts of the parent: line items, a post’s comments'],
   ['RESTRICT / NO ACTION', 'The delete fails while children exist', 'Records with their own duties: invoices, payouts'],
   ['SET NULL', 'Child kept, link cleared', 'Optional links: a comment’s author']],
  'Why a deletion needs tracking',
  ['12 services hold data for each user', 'Each finishes its step on the first try 99.5% of the time', 'Failures are independent', '2,000 deletions a day'],
  ['All 12 succeed first time: 0.995^12 ≈ 0.942.',
   'So about 5.8% of deletions have at least one failed step.',
   '2,000 × 5.8% ≈ 117 deletions a day need a retry.',
   'Without a status per step, each of those silently leaves data behind.'],
  'Small failure rates add up across many services. Track each step, retry automatically, and alert only on what retries cannot fix.',
  'Deleting a workspace in a project-management app', 'Illustrative example',
  "Illustrative: a project-management app let admins delete a workspace, and the first version ran one cascading DELETE. Large customers’ deletions timed out and rolled back, and smaller ones still left files in object storage and documents in the search index. The rebuild marked the workspace deleting, ran a resumable batch job leaf-first, published WorkspaceDeleted through the outbox, and tracked acknowledgements from search, files and billing. Billing anonymised invoices instead of deleting them. Tombstones stopped an offline mobile client from re-uploading tasks a week later.",
  [('“Just put ON DELETE CASCADE everywhere.”', '“I’d choose per relationship: cascade parts, restrict what we must keep, and batch big deletes.”'),
   ('“We publish an event, so every service deletes the user.”', '“I’d track each service’s acknowledgement, retry failures, and keep a tombstone so late messages cannot recreate the data.”')],
  ['Choose ON DELETE per relationship, and index every foreign-key column.', 'Stage large deletes: mark, delete children in batches, then the parent.',
   'Run cross-service deletion as a tracked, idempotent workflow.', 'Keep tombstones so retries, caches and sync cannot bring data back.']),
 {"kind": "predict", "title": "An offline phone syncs a deleted note", "prompt": "A user deletes a note on their laptop, and the server hard-deletes the row, keeping no record. Their phone, offline for a week, then syncs its copy, which includes the note. The sync endpoint upserts every note it receives. What happens?",
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "The note is recreated on the server and the laptop", "feedback": "Right: with no trace of the delete, the server cannot tell an old copy from a new note."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "The server rejects the note as deleted", "feedback": "Only if it had kept a tombstone; the hard delete left nothing to compare against."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "The phone deletes its own copy during sync", "feedback": "The phone needs a delete marker from the server, and none exists."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "Keep a tombstone with the id and deletion time, have sync send delete markers and drop upserts for tombstoned ids, and expire tombstones only after the longest time a client may stay offline."},
 see([(538, 'Large deletes run as resumable batch jobs with checkpoints and throttling'),
      (535, 'A deletion request is a long-running operation with an id and a status'),
      (238, 'The deletion event leaves through an outbox written in the same transaction')])))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 538 batch jobs over millions of rows
T.append(topic(538, 'batch-jobs-over-large-tables', R, 34, 'Batch Jobs Over Millions of Rows: Chunking, Checkpoints and Throttling',
 ['Process a large table in key-ordered chunks with a saved checkpoint',
  'Make each chunk idempotent so a crashed job can resume safely',
  'Throttle batch work so live traffic and replicas keep up'],
 3, 13, [236],
 [text(538, 1, 'One giant transaction does not scale',
   "Backfilling a new column on 50M rows with a single UPDATE holds locks for a long time, writes one enormous transaction to the log, makes replicas fall far behind, and if it fails at 90% it rolls back everything and starts again. The same job run as thousands of small transactions keeps each lock brief, lets replicas keep pace, and turns a failure into losing one chunk instead of hours of work.",
   ['chunking']),
  diagram(538, 2, 'Key-ordered chunks with a checkpoint',
   'Each pass picks up exactly where the last one stopped.', ['chunking', 'checkpoints'],
   ['Read the checkpoint: last id done = 1,240,000',
    'Select the next 2,000 rows WHERE id > 1,240,000 ORDER BY id',
    'Process them and write the results in one short transaction',
    'Save the chunk’s last id as the new checkpoint in that same transaction',
    'Pause if replica lag or live latency is over its limit, then repeat']),
  text(538, 3, 'Worked example: how long a backfill takes',
   "50M rows in chunks of 2,000 is 25,000 chunks. At 150 ms per chunk that is 3,750 s, about an hour; adding a 100 ms pause between chunks makes it 25,000 × 0.25 s = 6,250 s, about 1.7 hours. Selecting chunks with OFFSET instead would make each chunk skip all earlier rows: chunk 20,000 reads past 40M rows before returning 2,000. Key-ordered chunks start from the index every time, so the last chunk costs the same as the first.",
   ['chunking']),
  text(538, 4, 'Idempotent chunks make restarts dull',
   "A job can crash after writing a chunk but before saving its checkpoint, unless both happen in one transaction, and then that chunk runs again. Make the work safe to repeat: set values computed from the row (SET total = price × qty) rather than adding to them, add a condition such as WHERE total IS NULL, and use upserts with unique keys. For side effects such as sending emails, record a per-row sent marker in the same transaction as the work.",
   ['checkpoints', 'batch-idempotency']),
  text(538, 5, 'Trap: running flat out against the primary',
   "A backfill running at full speed can take the primary database’s disk and CPU, so live p99 latency rises and replicas fall behind, which users see as their own changes disappearing for a while. Throttle it: a fixed rate, pauses whenever replica lag passes a limit such as 1 second, backing off when live latency rises, and running in quieter hours. A job that takes a day without anyone noticing beats one that takes an hour and pages the on-call engineer.",
   ['throttling'])],
 [mc('t538-a1', 'chunking', 'Why run a 50M-row backfill in chunks of a few thousand rows?',
   [('a', 'Chunked updates use less disk in total'), ('b', 'Short transactions keep locks and lag small'),
    ('c', 'Chunks let the job skip rows it does not need'), ('d', 'Smaller chunks reduce the total work done')], 'b',
   'a: the total data written is about the same. b: correct, each transaction is brief, replicas keep up, and a failure loses only one chunk. c: which rows are touched depends on the WHERE clause, not the chunking. d: chunking adds a little overhead; its value is safety, not less work.'),
  mc('t538-a2', 'chunking', 'Why select each chunk with WHERE id > last_id rather than OFFSET?',
   [('a', 'OFFSET returns rows in a random order'), ('b', 'OFFSET cannot be combined with ORDER BY'),
    ('c', 'Each chunk starts from the index, not by skipping'), ('d', 'Keyset queries take no locks of any kind')], 'c',
   'a: with ORDER BY, OFFSET is ordered too; its cost is the problem. b: OFFSET is normally used with ORDER BY. c: correct, a keyset query seeks straight to the next key, while OFFSET reads and discards every earlier row, so late chunks get slower and slower. d: the writes in each chunk still take row locks.'),
  mc('t538-a3', 'checkpoints', 'A job commits each chunk, then saves its checkpoint in a separate transaction, and crashes between the two. What happens on restart?',
   [('a', 'The last chunk is skipped'), ('b', 'The last chunk is processed again'),
    ('c', 'The whole job restarts from row one'), ('d', 'The database rolls back both writes')], 'b',
   'a: the checkpoint still points before the chunk, so nothing is skipped. b: correct, the work is redone, which is safe only if the chunk is idempotent; saving the checkpoint in the same transaction avoids it. c: the checkpoint is only one chunk behind. d: the chunk’s transaction had already committed.'),
  mc('t538-a4', 'batch-idempotency', 'Which chunk update is safe to run twice?',
   [('a', 'SET total = total + price × qty'), ('b', 'SET visits = visits + 1'),
    ('c', 'INSERT a copy of each row into an archive with no key'), ('d', 'SET total = price × qty')], 'd',
   'a: running it twice adds the amount twice. b: every rerun increments again. c: without a unique key, a rerun inserts duplicates. d: correct, it sets the value from the row itself, so a second run writes the same result.'),
  mc('t538-a5', 'throttling', 'During a backfill, replica lag climbs to 40 s and users stop seeing their own changes. What should the job do?',
   [('a', 'Pause until lag is under a limit, then resume'), ('b', 'Use bigger chunks to finish sooner'),
    ('c', 'Switch to one large transaction'), ('d', 'Keep going; the lag clears when it ends')], 'a',
   'a: correct, lag-aware throttling lets replicas catch up and keeps the job running safely. b: bigger chunks make each transaction heavier and lag worse. c: one huge transaction is the worst case for replicas. d: users suffer for the whole run, which may be hours.'),
  order('t538-a6', 'checkpoints', 'Order one pass of a resumable batch job.',
   ['Read the last saved checkpoint', 'Fetch the next chunk after it, in key order', 'Write the results in a short transaction',
    'Save the chunk’s last key as the checkpoint', 'Check replica lag and pause if needed'],
   ['s3', 's5', 's1', 's4', 's2'],
   'The checkpoint brackets the work: read it first, advance it only after the chunk is written, then let the throttle decide when the next pass starts.'),
  est('t538-a7', 'chunking', 'A job updates 12M rows in chunks of 4,000, and each chunk takes 0.3 s including its pause. About how many minutes does it run?', 'minutes', 15,
   ['Chunk time stays steady', 'No restarts'], '12M ÷ 4,000 × 0.3 s ÷ 60', '3,000 chunks × 0.3 s = 900 s = 15 minutes',
   'Sanity check: with OFFSET-based chunks the late chunks would scan millions of skipped rows each, and the same job could take many hours.', 0.1, [1.5, 150])],
 flash(538, [('Why chunk a backfill?', 'Short transactions keep locks, log size and replica lag small, and failures cheap.'),
             ('Keyset chunk query?', 'WHERE id > last_id ORDER BY id LIMIT n, so every chunk starts from the index.'),
             ('How is a batch kept from hurting live traffic?', 'Throttle on replica lag and live latency, with pauses and a rate limit.')]),
 src([('PostgreSQL: Populating a database', 'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/populate.html'),
      ('PostgreSQL: Monitoring replication (pg_stat_replication)', 'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html'),
      ('Google SRE workbook: Data processing pipelines', 'https://sre.google/workbook/data-processing/')]),
 deep(['chunking', 'checkpoints', 'batch-idempotency', 'throttling'], 'How a resumable batch job walks a big table',
  ["The job walks the table in primary-key order, or in any indexed, unique order. Each pass selects the next n rows after the last key it finished, with a condition such as WHERE id > :last ORDER BY id LIMIT 2000. Because the database seeks straight to that key in the index, every chunk costs about the same, whether it is the first or the twenty-thousandth.",
   "Each chunk is processed and written in one short transaction. The chunk size is chosen so a transaction takes well under a second, typically a few hundred to a few thousand rows, and tuned by measuring. Small enough to keep locks brief and replicas close behind; large enough that per-transaction overhead does not dominate.",
   "The checkpoint, the last key finished, is saved in a small job table, ideally in the same transaction as the chunk’s writes, so the two can never disagree. On restart the job reads the checkpoint and continues. If the checkpoint is saved separately, a crash can repeat one chunk, so the chunk’s work must be idempotent anyway: set values rather than increment them, filter for rows not yet done, and upsert on unique keys.",
   "Between chunks the job checks the health of the system it is loading: replica lag, the primary’s CPU or I/O, and the p99 latency of live queries. If any is over its limit, it sleeps and checks again. A rate cap, a time-of-day window and a kill switch that stops it within one chunk give operators control without killing processes.",
   "The job reports progress: the checkpoint, rows done, rows per second and an estimated finish time. It ends with a verification step, such as counting rows still not done, which must be zero. For schema changes the usual order is to add the new column, deploy code that writes it for new rows, backfill old rows with the job, verify, and only then make the column required or start reading from it."],
  ['Backfills, data migrations, re-encryption, recomputing derived columns.', 'Any job touching more rows than one transaction should hold.'],
  ['Avoid OFFSET to page through a big table; late pages get slower and slower.', 'Avoid running batch work flat out on the primary during peak hours.'],
  ['Approach', 'Cost per chunk', 'Restart', 'Impact on live traffic'],
  [['One big UPDATE', 'One huge transaction', 'From the start', 'Long locks and replica lag'],
   ['OFFSET chunks', 'Grows with position', 'From a page number', 'Rising load as it goes'],
   ['Keyset chunks with checkpoint and throttle', 'Flat', 'From the last key', 'Bounded by the throttle']],
  'OFFSET versus keyset over a large table',
  ['50M rows', 'Chunks of 2,000, so 25,000 chunks', 'OFFSET reads and discards every earlier row'],
  ['Keyset: each chunk reads about 2,000 rows, so about 50M row reads in total.',
   'OFFSET: chunk k first skips (k − 1) × 2,000 rows; on average about 25M skipped rows per chunk.',
   'Total for OFFSET: about 25,000 × 25M ≈ 6 × 10^11 row reads, roughly 12,500 times the keyset total.',
   'Even at millions of rows read per second, that is days of extra work.'],
  'Key-ordered chunks keep a long job’s cost linear in the table size; OFFSET makes it quadratic.',
  'Backfilling a column in a large payments table', 'Illustrative example',
  "Illustrative: a payments company adds a normalised merchant category to a table of several hundred million transactions. The team adds a nullable column, deploys code that fills it for new rows, then runs a backfill in chunks of 2,000 by primary key, saving the checkpoint with each chunk and pausing whenever replica lag passes one second. The job runs for most of a day at a rate nobody notices. A final count of rows still null confirms it finished before the column is made required.",
  [('“We will run one UPDATE over the weekend.”', '“I’d chunk it by key with a checkpoint, so it can pause, resume and never lag the replicas.”'),
   ('“If it crashes we will rerun it.”', '“I’d make each chunk idempotent, so reruns are safe and it resumes from the checkpoint.”')],
  ['Walk big tables in key order, in short transactions.', 'Save a checkpoint with each chunk so the job resumes, not restarts.',
   'Make chunk work idempotent: set rather than increment.', 'Throttle on replica lag and live latency.']),
 {"kind": "tradeoff", "title": "Backfilling 80M rows on a live database", "prompt": "Pick the best fit for these constraints.",
  "setup": ['80M rows on the primary database need a new column filled', 'Users read from replicas and expect their changes within 2 s', 'The job may take a day'],
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "Keyset chunks with a checkpoint, paused when replica lag passes 1 s", "feedback": "Right: steady, resumable progress that never lets replicas fall behind what users notice."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "A single UPDATE statement run in a quiet hour", "feedback": "One 80M-row transaction causes heavy lag even when quiet, and a late failure rolls back everything."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "Many parallel workers at full speed, to finish quickly", "feedback": "Finishes sooner but floods the primary’s I/O, and replicas fall well behind the 2 s promise."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "When the job may take a day, use the day. Throttling on replica lag keeps the user-visible promise, checkpoints make restarts cheap, and key-ordered chunks keep every chunk equally fast."},
 see([(236, 'Keyset chunks are keyset pagination applied to a job instead of a screen'),
      (272, 'A backfill is the middle step of an expand-and-contract schema change'),
      (356, 'Throttling a batch job is backpressure applied to your own work'),
      (227, 'Long backfills usually run as background jobs with retries and progress'),
      (537, 'Deleting a large object’s children is a batch job like any other')])))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 539 audit trails
T.append(topic(539, 'audit-trails-in-a-service', R, 35, 'Audit Trails in Code: Actors, Impersonation and Activity Logs',
 ['Carry the real actor, and the account they act for, from the request into every audit record, including jobs and service calls',
  'Design support impersonation so every action is attributable, time-limited and tied to a reason',
  'Record audits in shared code, including denials, and serve customer activity logs from the same records'],
 2, 12, [225],
 [text(539, 1, 'An actor context, set once per request',
   "Handlers should not build audit records from scratch. The middleware that authenticates a request creates an actor context: the actor’s id and type (user, staff, service or API key), the account acted on behalf of, the tenant, the request id and the source address. The audit helper reads that context, so every record has the same shape and nobody forgets the actor. Background jobs and calls to other services carry the context forward, in the job payload or in a token the receiver can verify.",
   ['actor-context']),
  text(539, 2, 'Worked example: who refunded order 81?',
   "A support agent clicks Refund; the API queues a job; the worker calls the payments service with its own credentials. If each hop records only its immediate caller, the payments service’s record says refund-worker refunded order 81, and the agent’s name stops at the API. Put the original actor (agent 17, acting for customer 5523, ticket SUP-4410) in the job payload and pass it on the service call, and have every hop record both: “caller refund-worker, initiated by agent 17 for customer 5523, ticket SUP-4410”.",
   ['actor-context']),
  diagram(539, 3, 'Support impersonation with an audit trail',
   'Acting as a customer is a separate, short-lived kind of session, never a shared login.', ['impersonation'],
   ['The agent asks to act as a customer and gives a ticket number',
    'The system checks the agent’s role and, for sensitive scopes, asks a second person to approve',
    'It issues a short-lived session marked actor = agent, subject = customer',
    'Every action in that session is recorded with both ids and the ticket',
    'The session expires, and the customer’s activity log shows that support accessed the account']),
  text(539, 4, 'Activity logs customers can see',
   "Many products show users and their admins a trail of their own: “New sign-in from Chrome on Windows, Pune”, “Asha changed the billing email”. Build these from the same audit records, filtered by tenant and permission, with action names turned into readable sentences and staff shown by role, such as “Support”, where policy says so. Enterprise customers often want an export or an API to feed their own security tools; scope it to their tenant and page it by time and id, not by offset.",
   ['activity-logs']),
  text(539, 5, 'Trap: one audit call per handler, written by hand',
   "When every handler calls audit() itself after its work succeeds, some forget, and denied attempts are never recorded at all, because the handler returns its 403 before reaching the call. Put auditing in shared code: the authorisation layer records allow and deny decisions for sensitive actions, and the data-access layer records changes in the same transaction as the write. Thirty refused refund attempts from one account in a minute is exactly what an investigator wants to find.",
   ['audit-coverage'])],
 [mc('t539-a1', 'actor-context', 'Refunds are issued by a queued job, and every refund in the audit trail shows the actor “refund-worker”. What fixes this?',
   [('a', 'Run the worker under each agent’s own database login'), ('b', 'Carry the original actor in the job payload and record it'),
    ('c', 'Log the agent’s id to the debug log when queuing'), ('d', 'Find the agent later by matching up timestamps')], 'b',
   'a: database logins per person do not scale and still lose who acted for whom. b: correct, the job knows who asked for it, and every record can name both the agent and the worker. c: debug logs are sampled, rotated and not joined to the audit record. d: matching by time is guesswork the moment two agents act in the same second.'),
  mc('t539-a2', 'impersonation', 'A support agent signs in as a customer to debug an issue and changes the customer’s address. Who should the record show as the actor?',
   [('a', 'The customer, as it was their session'), ('b', 'The agent, acting on behalf of the customer'),
    ('c', 'The support tool’s service account'), ('d', 'Nobody, as it was only debugging')], 'b',
   'a: the customer did not make the change, and a dispute would blame the wrong person. b: correct, record the real actor and the account they acted for. c: a shared service account hides which person acted. d: changes made while debugging are still changes to a customer’s data.'),
  mc('t539-a3', 'impersonation', 'Which setup best fits support staff who sometimes need to see a customer’s account?',
   [('a', 'A shared support login that can open any account'), ('b', 'A short session tied to a ticket, naming both people'),
    ('c', 'The customer’s password, sent to support over chat'), ('d', 'Permanent admin rights for every support agent')], 'b',
   'a: a shared login cannot say which agent acted, and its access never ends. b: correct, the session expires, has a stated reason, and every action in it records the agent and the customer. c: it teaches customers to share passwords and makes the agent indistinguishable from the customer. d: standing admin rights give every agent far more access than any one ticket needs.'),
  mc('t539-a4', 'audit-coverage', 'Refund handlers call audit() after a refund succeeds. Which events will the trail miss?',
   [('a', 'Refund attempts the policy check denied'), ('b', 'Refunds above a set amount, such as ₹10,000'),
    ('c', 'Refunds issued by admins rather than agents'), ('d', 'Refunds that took longer than a second')], 'a',
   'a: correct, a denied request returns before the audit call, so probing and abuse leave no trace; recording decisions in the authorisation layer fixes it. b: large refunds succeed like any other and are recorded. c: the handler runs the same way whoever the actor is. d: slow requests still reach the audit call when they succeed.'),
  mc('t539-a5', 'activity-logs', 'An enterprise customer wants to pull its own audit records into its security tools every day. What fits best?',
   [('a', 'A CSV that support emails to them on request'), ('b', 'An API scoped to their tenant, paged by time and id'),
    ('c', 'Read access to the shared audit table'), ('d', 'A daily copy of the application’s debug logs')], 'b',
   'a: manual exports are slow, irregular and easy to send to the wrong person. b: correct, tenant scoping keeps other customers’ records out, and keyset paging stays fast however far back the export reaches. c: the shared table holds every tenant’s records. d: debug logs are incomplete, unstructured for this purpose, and may contain other tenants’ data.'),
  order('t539-a6', 'impersonation', 'Order how a support agent gets to act as a customer.',
   ['The agent requests access and gives a ticket number', 'The system checks the agent’s role and any approval needed',
    'A short-lived session is issued with actor and subject set', 'Each action in it is recorded with both ids and the ticket',
    'The session expires and appears in the customer’s activity log'],
   ['s4', 's1', 's5', 's3', 's2'],
   'A reason comes first, authorisation before any access, and the session itself carries both identities, so every record written during it names the real actor.'),
  est('t539-a7', 'activity-logs', 'A tenant exports 30 million audit records through an API that returns 1,000 records per page, each page taking 0.2 s with keyset paging. How many minutes does the full export take?', 'minutes', 100,
   ['Pages are fetched one after another', 'Each page takes the same time'], '30M ÷ 1,000 × 0.2 s ÷ 60', '30,000 pages × 0.2 s = 6,000 s = 100 minutes',
   'Sanity check: with offset paging each page would skip all earlier rows and grow slower, so the same export would take many times longer; keyset paging keeps every page at 0.2 s.', 0.1, [10, 1000])],
 flash(539, [('Actor context?', 'Set once by authentication: actor, on-behalf-of, tenant, request id; carried into jobs and service calls.'),
             ('Impersonation done right?', 'A short-lived, ticket-linked session that records both the agent and the customer.'),
             ('Where should denied attempts be recorded?', 'In the authorisation layer, so a 403 is audited without each handler remembering.')]),
 src([('OWASP: Logging cheat sheet', 'https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html'),
      ('OWASP: Logging Vocabulary cheat sheet', 'https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Vocabulary_Cheat_Sheet.html'),
      ('RFC 8693: OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (the act claim)', 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8693')]),
 deep(['actor-context', 'impersonation', 'audit-coverage', 'activity-logs'], 'How a service attributes every action to someone',
  ["The actor context is created where authentication happens. It names the actor and its type (a person, a staff member, a service, an API key), the account acted on behalf of, the tenant, the request id and the source address. Code reads it from request-scoped context; nothing below the middleware asks the caller who they are.",
   "The context travels. Within a process it is request-scoped; across a queue it goes in the job payload; across services it goes in a token or header the receiver can verify. OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange, for example, defines an act claim for “this service is acting for that user”. Each record keeps both the immediate caller and the originating actor.",
   "Audits are written by shared code with stable action names such as refund.issue or role.grant. The authorisation layer records allow and deny decisions for sensitive actions; the data-access layer records changes in the same transaction as the write, so neither exists without the other. Handlers add only what is specific to them, such as the amount or the reason.",
   "Impersonation and emergency access are their own kind of session: requested with a reason or ticket, approved by someone else for sensitive scopes, limited to minutes, visibly marked in the interface, recorded with both identities, and reviewed afterwards. Many products also tell the customer, through their activity log or a notification.",
   "The same records serve readers. Customer-facing activity logs and exports are filtered by tenant and permission, translate action names into sentences, and page by time and id. Retention can differ by plan, and personal details stay out of the records, which refer to people by id, so a person’s data can be erased elsewhere while the trail stays intact."],
  ['Admin panels, support tools, money movements and access to personal data.', 'Systems with background jobs or several services acting on a user’s behalf.'],
  ['Avoid recording only the immediate caller, such as a worker or service account.', 'Avoid shared support logins and hand-written audit calls in each handler.'],
  ['Actor recorded', 'What an investigator learns', 'Gap'],
  [['Only the immediate caller', 'Which service made the call', 'Not who asked for it'],
   ['Only the signed-in session', 'Whose account it was', 'Not which agent was impersonating'],
   ['Originating actor, on-behalf-of and caller', 'Who, for whom, and through what', 'Must be carried across every hop']],
  'Sizing a customer activity log',
  ['20,000 tenants', 'About 5,000 audit records per tenant per month', 'About 600 bytes per record, indexes included', 'Plans keep 90 days or 1 year'],
  ['Per month: 20,000 × 5,000 = 100M records.',
   'In bytes: 100M × 600 bytes = 60 GB a month.',
   '90-day retention holds about 180 GB; 1-year retention about 720 GB.',
   'Keeping the last 90 days in fast storage and older months in cheaper storage keeps search quick for most readers.'],
  'An activity log is a product feature with a storage bill. Set retention per plan and keep recent months fast to search.',
  'Support access at a lending company', 'Illustrative example',
  "Illustrative: support staff at a lending app sometimes need to see a borrower’s screens. They open a ticket-linked session that lasts 30 minutes; anything sensitive, such as changing bank details, needs a second agent’s approval. Every action records the agent, the borrower and the ticket, and fee waivers queued from the session carry the same context into the billing worker. The borrower’s activity page shows “Support viewed your account (ticket SUP-4410)”, and a monthly review searches the trail for agents with unusual numbers of waivers.",
  [('“The worker made the change, so the actor is the worker.”', '“I’d record the worker as the caller and the agent who asked as the actor, carried in the job payload.”'),
   ('“Support can just log in as the customer.”', '“I’d use a short, ticket-linked impersonation session that records both the agent and the customer.”')],
  ['Build an actor context once per request and pass it into jobs and service calls.', 'Record both the originating actor and the immediate caller.',
   'Make impersonation short-lived, reasoned and visible in the record.', 'Audit in shared code, including denials, and serve activity logs from the same records.']),
 {"kind": "predict", "title": "A refund through a queue", "prompt": "An agent clicks Refund. The API queues a job, and the worker calls the payments service with its own service credentials. Each component writes an audit record naming whoever called it. Months later a customer disputes the refund. What does the payments service’s record say?",
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "The refund was made by refund-worker", "feedback": "Right: each hop saw only its immediate caller, so the agent’s name stopped at the API."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "The refund was made by the support agent", "feedback": "Only if the agent’s identity travelled with the job; here the worker used only its own credentials."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "The refund was made by the customer", "feedback": "The customer did not act, and nothing in the chain names them as the actor."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "Carry the originating actor, the account acted for and the ticket through the job payload and service calls, and record them next to the immediate caller at every hop."},
 see([(225, 'Authorisation decisions, including denials, are what audit records describe'),
      (229, 'Request ids tie audit records to the logs and traces of the same request'),
      (236, 'Activity-log exports page by time and id, not by offset')])))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 540 holding many live connections
T.append(topic(540, 'holding-many-live-connections', R, 36, 'Holding Many Live Connections on the Server',
 ['Estimate the memory and file descriptors that many long-lived connections need',
  'Deliver a message to a user connected to another server through pub/sub',
  'Survive deploys and outages without a reconnect storm or lost messages'],
 3, 13, [356],
 [text(540, 1, 'A connection is a resource you hold',
   "Each open WebSocket or server-sent events stream holds a file descriptor, kernel socket buffers and some application state for as long as the user stays connected. At an estimated 20 KB per connection, 100,000 connections need about 2 GB on one server. Raise the per-process file-descriptor limit (a soft limit of 1,024 is a common default), and use event-driven I/O so one thread serves many sockets instead of a thread per connection.",
   ['connection-cost']),
  diagram(540, 2, 'Reaching a user on another server',
   'Gateways hold the sockets; a pub/sub layer connects them.', ['cross-node-fanout'],
   ['User A is connected to gateway 3, user B to gateway 7',
    'A’s message reaches the chat service through gateway 3',
    'The service publishes it to B’s channel in a pub/sub system',
    'Gateway 7, subscribed to B’s channel, writes it down B’s connection',
    'If B is offline, it waits in storage until B reconnects and asks for it']),
  text(540, 3, 'Worked example: a reconnect storm',
   "A gateway holding 200,000 connections restarts. If every client reconnects within about a second, the fleet faces 200,000 TLS handshakes and authentications at once. Against an estimated capacity of 10,000 a second, that is a 20 s backlog; clients time out and retry, adding still more. If clients instead wait a random delay spread over 60 s before reconnecting, the load is about 3,300 a second, well within capacity.",
   ['reconnect-storm']),
  text(540, 4, 'Heartbeats and graceful drains',
   "Load balancers and home routers silently drop connections that look idle, and the server gets no close event. Send a ping every 30 s or so and close the connection if two in a row go unanswered. For deploys, drain instead of killing: take the gateway out of the load balancer, send connected clients a reconnect message that tells each to wait a random delay, give them a few minutes to move, then close what remains and stop.",
   ['heartbeats', 'draining']),
  text(540, 5, 'Trap: treating the socket as the delivery guarantee',
   "A message written to a socket can still be lost if the connection dies before the client reads it. Give each message a per-conversation sequence number and store it before sending. The client remembers the last number it processed and, on reconnecting, asks for everything after it; the server replays from storage. Live delivery is the fast path, and the replay on reconnect is what makes delivery reliable.",
   ['resume'])],
 [mc('t540-a1', 'connection-cost', 'A gateway holds 250,000 connections at about 16 KB each. Roughly how much memory do the connections use?',
   [('a', 'About 400 MB'), ('b', 'About 4 GB'), ('c', 'About 16 GB'), ('d', 'About 40 GB')], 'b',
   'a: that is ten times too small. b: correct, 250,000 × 16 KB = 4,000,000 KB, about 4 GB. c: that would be 64 KB each. d: ten times too large.'),
  mc('t540-a2', 'cross-node-fanout', 'User B is connected to a different server from user A. How does A’s message reach B?',
   [('a', 'A’s server opens a socket to B’s device'), ('b', 'B’s client polls A’s server every second'),
    ('c', 'B’s server receives it through pub/sub'), ('d', 'The load balancer copies it to every server')], 'c',
   'a: devices accept no inbound connections; they connect out to gateways. b: B does not know which server A uses, and polling every second defeats the open connection. c: correct, B’s gateway subscribes to B’s channel and pushes what is published there. d: load balancers route requests; they do not broadcast messages to servers.'),
  mc('t540-a3', 'reconnect-storm', 'After a gateway restart, every dropped client reconnects at once and the replacement gateway falls over. What prevents this?',
   [('a', 'Longer heartbeat intervals on the server'), ('b', 'Reconnecting at once to another gateway'),
    ('c', 'A higher file-descriptor limit'), ('d', 'A random, growing delay before reconnects')], 'd',
   'a: heartbeats detect dead connections; they do not spread out reconnects. b: moving the whole crowd to another gateway moves the overload with it. c: the bottleneck is handshakes per second, not open descriptors. d: correct, jittered backoff spreads the reconnects over time so the fleet absorbs them.'),
  mc('t540-a4', 'heartbeats', 'Idle connections die after a few minutes with no close event on the server. What is the usual fix?',
   [('a', 'Raise the server’s read timeout to an hour'), ('b', 'Have every client reconnect each minute'),
    ('c', 'Ping every 30 s; close if pongs stop'), ('d', 'Switch from server-sent events to polling')], 'c',
   'a: the middlebox drops the idle connection regardless of the server’s timeout. b: constant reconnects add handshake load for no reason. c: correct, regular pings keep the path active and reveal dead connections so the server can free them. d: polling avoids idle sockets but gives up the push model and adds request load.'),
  mc('t540-a5', 'resume', 'A phone loses signal for 20 s during a chat. How does it get the messages it missed?',
   [('a', 'It reconnects and asks for all after its last id'), ('b', 'The server resends everything since login'),
    ('c', 'The socket holds them until signal returns'), ('d', 'It does not; chats show only live messages')], 'a',
   'a: correct, sequence numbers let the client ask for exactly the gap, which the server replays from storage. b: resending everything wastes bandwidth and duplicates what was already shown. c: the old connection is dead; its buffers are gone. d: users expect to see what was sent while they were away.'),
  order('t540-a6', 'draining', 'Order a graceful deploy of one connection gateway.',
   ['Stop sending it new connections at the load balancer', 'Send connected clients a reconnect hint with a random delay',
    'Wait a few minutes while clients move', 'Close the connections that remain', 'Stop the process and start the new version'],
   ['s4', 's2', 's5', 's1', 's3'],
   'Draining moves clients over minutes, at a pace the other gateways can absorb, instead of dropping them all at once.'),
  est('t540-a7', 'reconnect-storm', '200,000 dropped clients reconnect with jitter, spread evenly over 50 s. About how many handshakes per second must the fleet absorb?', 'handshakes/s', 4000,
   ['Reconnects are spread evenly', 'Each client reconnects once'], '200,000 ÷ 50 s', '200,000 ÷ 50 = 4,000 handshakes/s',
   'Sanity check: with no jitter all 200,000 arrive within about a second, fifty times this rate.', 0.1, [200000, 40000])],
 flash(540, [('Cost of a live connection?', 'A file descriptor, socket buffers and app state, held for as long as it is open.'),
             ('Reaching a user on another gateway?', 'Publish to the user’s channel; the gateway holding their socket subscribes and pushes.'),
             ('Avoiding a reconnect storm?', 'Jittered, growing reconnect delays, and draining gateways gradually on deploy.')]),
 src([('RFC 6455: The WebSocket Protocol', 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455'),
      ('MDN: Using server-sent events', 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Server-sent_events/Using_server-sent_events'),
      ('man7.org: epoll(7)', 'https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/epoll.7.html')]),
 deep(['connection-cost', 'cross-node-fanout', 'reconnect-storm', 'heartbeats', 'draining', 'resume'], 'How a gateway fleet holds connections and delivers messages',
  ["Clients open a long-lived connection to a gateway: a WebSocket for two-way traffic or a server-sent events stream for server-to-client updates. The gateway authenticates the connection once, records which user it belongs to, and keeps it open. Gateways do little else, so they can be scaled and restarted separately from the services that hold the business logic.",
   "Each gateway serves many sockets from a few threads with event-driven I/O, such as epoll on Linux, which tells the process which sockets are ready instead of parking a thread on each. Its capacity is set by memory per connection, the file-descriptor limit, CPU for heartbeats and messages, and above all how many new handshakes it can complete per second.",
   "When a service wants to reach a user, it does not need to know which gateway holds them. It publishes to the user’s channel in a pub/sub system, and the gateway holding that user subscribes to the channel while the connection is open. Messages are also written to storage with a sequence number before publishing, so a client that was away can catch up.",
   "Connections fail all the time: phones change networks, routers drop idle flows, gateways restart. Heartbeats find dead connections so their resources are freed. Clients reconnect with exponential backoff and random jitter, and on reconnecting they send the last sequence number they processed, so the server replays only the gap. Delivery is at least once, so clients ignore duplicates by sequence number.",
   "Deploys and incidents are where fleets break. A gateway being replaced is drained: removed from the load balancer, its clients told to reconnect after a random delay, then closed after a few minutes. Capacity is planned for the reconnect rate after losing a whole gateway, not only for the steady number of connections, and admission control sheds handshakes beyond what the fleet can complete."],
  ['Chat, live scores, notifications, collaborative editing and dashboards that push updates.', 'Any feature where users stay connected for minutes or hours.'],
  ['Avoid a thread per connection; memory and scheduling fall over long before sockets do.', 'Avoid relying on the open socket alone for delivery; store and replay by sequence number.'],
  ['Concern', 'Usual limit', 'Mitigation'],
  [['Memory per connection', 'RAM per gateway', 'Lean per-connection state, more gateways'],
   ['New handshakes per second', 'TLS and auth CPU', 'Jittered reconnects, gradual drains'],
   ['Cross-gateway delivery', 'Pub/sub throughput', 'Channels per user or room, sharded pub/sub']],
  'Sizing a gateway',
  ['A 32 GB gateway, keeping half its RAM free for headroom', 'About 16 KB per connection (an estimate)', 'About 5,000 new handshakes per second per gateway (an estimate)'],
  ['Memory: 16 GB ÷ 16 KB ≈ 1,000,000 connections.',
   'If that gateway dies, its clients must reconnect elsewhere. Spread over 60 s, that is about 16,700 handshakes per second.',
   'At about 5,000 handshakes per second per gateway, absorbing it takes about 3.3 gateways’ worth of spare handshake capacity.',
   'So a fleet often holds far fewer connections per gateway than memory allows, such as 200,000, to keep failures absorbable.'],
  'Memory says how many connections a gateway can hold; the reconnect rate after a failure says how many it should hold.',
  'Live scores during a big match', 'Illustrative example',
  "Illustrative: a sports app pushes ball-by-ball updates to millions of fans during a cricket final. Each fan’s app holds a connection to a gateway subscribed to the match channel. When a wicket falls, many more users open the app at once, so the team pre-scales gateways before the match, uses jittered reconnects, and drains gateways slowly if one must be replaced mid-match. Updates carry sequence numbers, so an app that dropped off in a tunnel fetches exactly the balls it missed.",
  [('“WebSockets scale to millions; we will just open them.”', '“Each one costs memory and a descriptor, and a failed gateway causes a reconnect storm, so I’d size for that.”'),
   ('“If we wrote it to the socket, the user got it.”', '“The connection can die first, so I’d store messages with sequence numbers and replay the gap on reconnect.”')],
  ['Each live connection holds memory and a file descriptor.', 'Pub/sub lets any service reach a user on any gateway.',
   'Jittered reconnects and gradual drains prevent reconnect storms.', 'Sequence numbers and replay on reconnect make delivery reliable.']),
 {"kind": "predict", "title": "Ten gateways, no pub/sub", "prompt": "A chat service runs 10 gateways and users are assigned to them at random. Each gateway delivers a message only if the recipient is connected to that same gateway. What do users see?",
  "choices": [{"id": "a", "label": "Only about 1 message in 10 arrives live", "feedback": "Right: sender and recipient share a gateway only about a tenth of the time."},
              {"id": "b", "label": "Every message arrives, a little more slowly", "feedback": "Nothing carries messages between gateways, so most never reach the recipient’s socket."},
              {"id": "c", "label": "Every message arrives twice", "feedback": "Duplicates come from retries, not from missing cross-gateway delivery."}],
  "correctId": "a",
  "reveal": "Connections are spread over many servers, so delivery needs a layer that finds the right one: pub/sub channels per user or room, or a lookup of which gateway holds each user. Store messages too, so the ones that miss live delivery are fetched on reconnect."},
 see([(10, 'Polling, server-sent events and WebSockets compared at system scale'),
      (218, 'The client side of the same connection: reconnecting and resuming in the browser'),
      (356, 'Admission control and load shedding protect gateways during reconnect storms')])))

